  "id": "ch2-voltagedivider",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "ch2-voltagedivider.html",
  "type": "Section",
  "number": "2.5",
  "title": "Voltage Divider",
  "body": " Voltage Divider   The voltage divider is a tool that uses Ohm's law to calculate the voltage dropped over resistive elements that are in series with each other without having to calculate current.  In the simplest case, consider a circuit containing a voltage source ( ) and two resistors ( and ), all wired in series. Ohm's law can be used to calculate the overall voltage\/current relationship of the circuit, and is shown in . (We are calculating current here only to show how to derive the voltage divider rule.)     Because everything is wired in series, this same current flows through both resistors. We can use Ohm's law to calculate the voltage drop over each resistor ( , and ), and plug in the result of to remove the current term from the equation. The results are shown in and .     In general, can be used to calculate the voltage dropped over the resistor of resistors in series (where ), given a source voltage of . As previously mentioned, there is no need to calculate current as long as the voltage and resistor values are known.      Using the voltage divider rule   Calculate in the circuit shown below.   A circuit used to demonstrate the voltage divider rule.   Circuit with 20.5V source, 28k, 20k, 32k, 38k, 27k, and 30k resistors. There are three meshes and five nodes. High potential of 20.5V, 20k, and 28k connect at node a. 20k and 32k connect at node b. 38k, 28k, and 27k connect at node c. 27k and 30k connect at node d. Low potential of 20.5V, 38k, and 30k connect at node e. Vout is defined as high potential at c and low potential at e.     Combine all resistors that are in series and re-draw the circuit.   A circuit used to demonstrate the voltage divider rule, with all series resistors combined.   Circuit with 20.5V source, 28k, 52k, 38k, and 57k resistors. Circuit has three meshes and three nodes. High potential of 20.5V, 28k, and 52k connect at node a. 52k, 28, 38, and 57k connect at node b. Low potential of 20.5V, 38k, and 57k connect at node c. Vout is defined as high potential at node b and low potential at node c.     The 28 kΩ and 52 kΩ resistors are in parallel with each other and have an equivalent resistance of 18.2 kΩ. The 38 kΩ and 57 kΩ resistors are in parallel with each other and have an equivalent resistance of 22.8 kΩ. The circuit can be re-drawn again.   A circuit used to demonstrate the voltage divider rule, with all series and parallel resistors combined.   Circuit with 20.5V, 18.2k, and 22.8k all in series. Vout is defined as dropping over 22.8k.     Use the voltage divider rule to calculate the output voltage.        Voltage divider with a two-mesh circuit   Calculate in the circuit shown below.   A circuit used to demonstrate the voltage divider rule in a two-mesh circuit.   Circuit with two meshes and four nodes containing one voltage source Vs and four resistors (R1, R2, R3, and R4). The high-potential end of Vs is connected to R1 at node a. R1, R2, and R3 are connected at node b. R3 and R4 are connected at node c. The low-potential end of Vs, R2, and R4 are connected at node d. Voltage Vx is measured over R2 from node b to node d. Voltage Vout is measured over R4 from node c to node d.     The voltage divider rule cannot immediately be applied to calculate . This is because the resistors are not all in series with the voltage source . However, the voltage divider rule can be applied directly to calculate , provided that the equivalent resistance seen by is calculated. This creates a circuit with two resistances in series with the voltage source.  The equivalent resistance seen by is equal to     Now, the voltage divider rule can be used to calculate .     Now that is known, the circuit can be re-drawn to represent as a source in series with resistors and .   The circuit with represented as a source.   Circuit with one mesh and three nodes containing a voltage source Vx and two resistors (R3 and R4). The high-potential end of Vx connects to R3 at node a. R3 and R4 connect at node b. The low-potential end of Vx connects to R4 at node c. Voltage Vout is measured over R4 from node b to node c.     Use the voltage divider rule to calculate the output voltage.     This process can be repeatedly iterated if there are three or more meshes in a circuit.      Wheatstone Bridge  A Wheatstone bridge is a measuring tool that can be used to indirectly determine the resistance of an object with otherwise unknown resistance. It is useful when measuring small resistances (or changes in resistance) that may not be within the accuracy of an ohmmeter. As will be demonstrated, the unknown resistance value is largely invariant to noise from the voltage source as its value is independent from the source. The schematic of a Wheatstone bridge is shown in , with the unknown resistance labeled .   Circuit schematic of a Wheatstone bridge circuit. is a variable resistor and is unknown.   Circuit with Vs, R1, R2, R3, Rx, and a voltmeter. Circuit has three meshes and four nodes. High potential of Vs, R1, and R3 connect at node a. R1, R2, and voltmeter connect at node b. R3, Rx, and voltmeter connect at node c. Low potential of Vs, R2, and Rx connect at node d. Voltage V1 is defined as high potential at node b and low potential at node d. V2 is defined as high potential at node c and low potential at node d.     The value of can be determined if the bridge is balanced. This occurs when the value of the variable resistor ( ) is changed such that the voltmeter reads 0 V. When the bridge is balanced, and are equal. The voltage divider rule can be used to calculate and . Then, set and equal to calculate . This is shown in and derived in .           "
