  "type": "Section",
  "number": "2.6",
  "title": "Current Divider",
  "body": " Current Divider  The current divider uses Ohm's law to calculate the current through resistive elements that are in parallel with each other without having to calculate voltage.  In the simplest case, consider a circuit containing a current source ( ) and two resistors ( and ), all wired in parallel. Ohm's law can be used to calculate the overall voltage\/current relationship of the circuit, and is shown in . (We are calculating voltage here only to show how to derive the current divider rule.)     Because the voltage dropped over both resistors is equal to , Ohm's law applied over each individual resistor can now be used to determine the individual currents through each resistor. Ohm's law states that the current through is equal to . The current through is equal to . Therefore, can be used to calculate the current through each resistor. This is shown in and .     Note the similarity with the voltage divider rule. Here we are using reciprocal resistances due to the parallel configuration of the resistors. In general, (derived below in ) can be used to calculate the current through the resistor of resistors in parallel (where ), given a source current of . As mentioned, there is no need to calculate the total voltage as long as the current and resistor values are known.           Using the current divider rule   The current through the load resistor ( ) shown in the circuit below must be 50 mA. Calculate the value of that is required to accomplish this.   A circuit used to demonstrate the current divider rule.   Circuit with 300mA source, 2k, 3k, and Rload resistors. All four circuit elements are in parallel. Node a is \"up\" and node b is \"down.\" The source flows \"up.\" The current through Rload is defined as flowing \"down.\"     Reduce the circuit by combining the 2 kΩ and 3 kΩ resistors in parallel. The circuit can be re-drawn.   A circuit used to demonstrate the current divider rule with the known resistors reduced to an equivalent resistance.   Circuit with 300mA source, 1.2k, and Rload resistors. All three circuit elements are in parallel. Node a is \"up\" and node b is \"down.\" The source flows \"up.\" The current through Rload is defined as flowing \"down.\"     Use the current divider rule to calculate the load resistance.        Current divider with a three-mesh circuit   Calculate in the circuit shown below.   A circuit used to demonstrate the current divider rule in a three-mesh circuit.   Circuit with 3 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of a current source Is and four resistors (R1, R2, R3, and R4). Is, R1, and R2 connect at node a. Is flows into node a. R2, R3, and R4 connect at node b. Is, R1, R3, and R4 connect at node c. Is flows out of node c. Branch current Ix flows through R2 from node a to node b. Branch current Iout flows through R4 from node b to node c.     The current divider rule cannot immediately be applied to calculate . This is because the resistors are not all in parallel with the current source. However, the current divider rule can be applied directly to calculate , provided that the equivalent resistance seen by is calculated. This creates a circuit with two resistances in parallel with the current source.  The equivalent resistance seen by is equal to     Now, the current divider rule can be used to calculate .     Now that is known, the circuit can be re-drawn to represent as a source in parallel with resistors and .   The circuit with represented as a source.   Circuit with two nodes and two meshes consisting of a current source Ix and resistors R3 and R4. All three circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. Current source Ix flows out of node b into node a. Branch current Iout flows through R4 from node a to node b.     Use the current divider rule to calculate the output voltage.     This process can be repeatedly iterated if there are four or more meshes in a circuit.    "
