  "body": " Demonstrating using KCL and KVL to form a matrix of equations   Consider the circuit shown in . There are four unknown currents, so four linearly independent equations will need to be derived to solve for each current. The direction of each current has been arbitrarily chosen.   Circuit used to demonstrate using KCL and KVL to solve for unknown currents.   Circuit with Vs, Is, R1, R2, R3, and R4. Circuit has three meshes and four nodes. High potential of Vs, R1, and R2 connect at a. Unknown current I1 flows out of a. Unknown current I2 flows out of a. R2, R3, and R4 connect at b. Unknown current I2 flows into b. Unknown current I3 flows out of b. Unknown current I4 flows out of b. R1, R4, and Is connect at c. Unknown current I1 flows into c. Unknown current I4 flows into c. Is flows out of c. Low potential of Vs, R3, and Is connect at d. Unknown current I3 flows into d. Is flows into d.     To find a sufficient number of linearly independent equations, analysis can occur at nodes (using KCL) and around loops (using KVL). It is important to try to find nodes at which we know (or want to know) all of the currents entering and exiting. In , there are four nodes, each labeled with a letter. This means we could generate four KCL equations (one at each node). However, by closely analyzing nodes a and d, we see that the current through the voltage source is unknown. Therefore, it would not be ideal to include nodes a or d in our set of equations. These are not perfect nodes.  The circuit in therefore has two perfect nodes: node b and node c. These nodes will be used solve KCL, giving us our first two linearly independent equations, shown in and .     We turn to KVL to find the remaining two equations. Just as we searched for perfect nodes to derive KCL equations, we will look for perfect loops to derive KVL equations. In , any loop that contains the current source would not be a perfect loop; we do not know (or care to find) the voltage dropped over the current source.  The loops that we choose must contain at least one unique circuit element from the others. For example (which is not necessarily relevant to the solution of this particular circuit): the loop containing , , and as well as the loop containing , , and are linearly independent. Including a third loop of , , , and would not add anything new to the circuit. The equations derived from these three loops would not lead to three linearly independent equations; they would only lead to two linearly independent equations.  Turning back to the example at hand: we desire two linearly independent equations that come from perfect loops. Two loops that satisfy those criteria are: the loop containing , , and and the loop containing , , and . Apply KVL around each loop. Use the direction of the current to determine if the voltage is a rise or a drop. Voltage rises will be negative, and voltage drops will be positive. The two equations are given in and .     Now that four linearly independent equations have been found, they can be put into the form of and placed into a matrix. Each of the equations, rewritten, are shown in .     The corresponding matrix is shown in . Now it can be solved to determine each of the individual currents.      "
