  "body": "  Calculate in the circuit shown in .   Circuit diagram corresponding to .   Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes containing CCCS of 0.2*IX, 320V source, 48k resistor, and 100k resistor. The CCCS, 48k, and 100k resistors connect at node A. The CCCS flows into node A. The 100k resistor and high-potential end of the 320V source connect at node B. The CCCS, 48k resistor, and low-potential end of the 320V source connect at node C. The CCCS flows out of node D. Ix is defined through the 48k resistor and flows out of node A into node C. Vx drops over the 48k resistor and drops from node A to node C.       120 V    As drawn, the circuit contains zero perfect nodes and zero perfect loops. To remedy this, the branch current through the 100 kΩ resistor will be defined. The re-drawn circuit is shown below.   Circuit diagram with all branch currents defined and the perfect node labeled.   Same circuit as above with I1 defined through the 100k resistor flowing out of node B into node C.     The circuit now has one perfect node and one perfect loop. There are two unknowns, so two equations will be derived. All units are in V, mA, and kΩ and will be omitted from the KCL and KVL equations to make them easier to read.  Node a is a perfect node. Using KCL at this node we obtain one equation.     The right mesh is a perfect loop. Using KVL around this loop (starting in the lower left and moving clockwise), we obtain the second equation.     The two equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of , the middle column contains coefficients of , and the right column contains constants.     The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.     Read the first row to determine that . Use Ohm's law to calculate .     This circuit can also be solved in a similar manner to . However, it may not be as intuitive. The equations below use units of V, kΩ, and mA and will not be explicitly shown. We know that   .  In addition, we know that   .  Use these three equations to solve for directly.      "
