  "number": "3.6",
  "title": "Norton’s Theorem",
  "body": " Norton's Theorem  Norton's theorem states that any linear circuit, regardless of the level of complexity, can be represented by an equivalent circuit that contains a current source in parallel with a resistor. This is depicted in .   Norton's theorem states that these two circuits are equivalent, given the correct value of and .   Left: a box representing an unknown linear circuit has two output terminals labeled a and b. Right: a current source of value In is wired in parallel with a resistor of value Rn. Terminal a is connected to the resistor and source (the source flows into terminal a). Terminal b is connected to the resistor and the source (the source flows out of terminal b).     The technique to Norton's theorem is in calculating the Norton equivalent current ( ) and Norton equivalent resistance ( ). The Norton equivalent current is equal to the short-circuit current between the output terminals (labeled a and b in ). The procedure to calculate the Norton equivalent resistance is given below.    If there are no dependent sources or op-amps in the circuit, then is equal to the equivalent resistance seen between the output terminals, with all of the independent sources deactivated.  If there are dependent sources or op-amps in the circuit, calculate the open-circuit voltage ( ) between the output terminals. The Norton equivalent resistance is equal to .    The test source method can also be used in solving Norton equivalent circuits for the same reasons that it would be used in solving Thévenin equivalent circuits.  It can be noted that Norton's theorem and Thévenin's theorem are functionally equivalent to each other; one is simply the source-transformed version of the other. It is therefore possible to find a Thévenin equivalent circuit and do a source transformation to derive the Norton equivalent circuit.   Norton equivalent circuit   Derive the Norton equivalent circuit between nodes a and b for the circuit shown in .   Circuit to be converted to a Norton equivalent circuit.   Circuit with 2 meshes and 5 nodes containing a 16V source, 4Ix CCCS, 4ohm, 12ohm, 5ohm, and 2ohm resistor. The 4ohm and 12ohm resistors connect at node A. The 5ohm resistor connects at node B. The high-potential end of the 16V source connects to the 4ohm resistor at node C. The 5ohm, 2ohm, and 4Ix CCCS connect at node D. The CCCS flows into node D. The low-potential end of the 16V source, 12ohm, 2ohm, and CCCS connect at node E. The CCCS flows out of node E. Ix is defined through the 12ohm resistor and flows out of node A and into node E.     Because there is a dependent source, cannot be calculated directly. We will need to calculate and . The open-circuit voltage is equal to     To calculate , note that the left side of the circuit that defines is disconnected from the rest of the circuit. This quantity can be calculated using the voltage divider rule.     To calculate , needs to be known. Ohm's law can be performed on the left side of the circuit to calculate this.     is equal to the voltage dropped over the 2 Ωresistor. Use Ohm's law to calculate this value.     can now be calculated.     Now we must short terminals a and b to calculate the Norton equivalent current. The circuit diagram is shown in .   Circuit with terminals a and b shorted to calculate .   Circuit with 3 meshes and 5 nodes containing a 16V source, 4Ix CCCS, 4ohm, 12ohm, 5ohm, and 2ohm resistor. The 4ohm, 12ohm, and 5ohm resistors connect at node A. The high-potential end of the 16V source connects to the 4ohm resistor at node C. The 5ohm, 2ohm, and 4Ix CCCS connect at node D. The CCCS flows into node D. The low-potential end of the 16V source, 12ohm, 2ohm, and CCCS connect at node E. The CCCS flows out of node E. Ix is defined through the 12ohm resistor and flows out of node A and into node E. In is defined through the 5ohm resistor and flows out of node A and into node D.     Mesh analysis will be the simplest method to solve this circuit. (There are fewer meshes than branches, making mesh analysis simpler than KCL\/KVL. Source transformation, while it could be used to reduce the circuit somewhat, cannot be used to reduce the circuit to one where a simple voltage divider or Ohm's law can be used. This is because branch currents are defined through resistors that would be modified in the source transformation process.)  KVL will be performed on each of the three meshes (clockwise mesh currents are numbered 1 3 from left to right), and the dependent source relationship will be used as a fourth equations. These equations are listed below.     The four equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of , then , , and . The right column contains constants.     The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.     The Norton equivalent current is equal to mesh current . Therefore, is 0.5 A.  Use Ohm's law to calculate the Norton equivalent resistance.     The Norton equivalent circuit is shown in .   Norton equivalent circuit.   Circuit with one mesh and two nodes containing a 0.5A source and 8ohm resistor. The source and resistor connect at node A. The source flows into node A. The source and resistor connect at node B. The source flows into node B.       "
