  "type": "Section",
  "number": "3.7",
  "title": "Maximum Power Transfer",
  "body": " Maximum Power Transfer  The theorem of maximum power transfer states that the maximum amount of power will be delivered to the load when the resistance of the load is equal to the Thévenin equivalent resistance of the circuit. The circuit shown in will be used to demonstrate this theorem.   Circuit diagram used to demonstrate the theorem of maximum power transfer.   Circuit containing a voltage source of value Vth wired in series with a resistor of value Rth and another resistor of value Rload. Current I flows through the circuit from high-potential to low-potential.     To determine the value of the resistance of the load ( ) that will lead to maximum power transfer, the power consumed by the load ( ) can be calculated using . This is shown in and derived in .      Ohm's law is used to calculate and the voltage divider rule is used to calculate .      The derivative of can be taken with respect to . This equation can be set equal to zero to calculate the maximum of the equation. (The full derivation will not be shown in this book.) Maximum power transfer occurs when is equal to .  The maximum power that can be consumed by the load can then be calculated. This value is defined in and derived in .          It is important to note that while the maximum amount of power is transferred when the load resistance is equal to the Thévenin resistance, this does not equate to the maximum load power efficiency (ratio of power delivered to the load to the total power). In the case of maximum power transfer, the power efficiency is limited to 50%.   Maximum power transfer   Calculate the value of the load resistor ( ) that is required for maximum power transfer given the circuit shown in . Then, calculate the maximum power transferred to that load.   Circuit for calculating maximum power transfer.   Circuit with 3 meshes and 4 nodes containing a 24V source, 4k resistor, 2Ix CCCS, 8k resistor, 2k resistor, and Rload resistor. The high-potential end of the 24V source connects to the 4k resistor at node A. The 4k resistor, 2Ix CCCS, and 8k resistor connect at node B. The CCCS flows into node B. The 8k, 2k, and Rload resistors connect at node C. The low-potential end of the 24V source, 2Ix CCCS, 2k, and Rload resistors connect at node D. The CCCS flows out of node D. Current Ix is defined through the 2k resistor and flows out of node C and into node D.     Convert the circuit to a Thévenin equivalent circuit. Use source transformation on the 24 V voltage source to convert it to a 6 mA current source. The source transformed circuit is shown in .   Source transformed circuit.   Circuit with 3 meshes and 3 nodes containing a 6mA source, 4k resistor, 2Ix CCCS, 8k resistor, and 2k resistor. The 6mA source, 4k resistor, 2Ix CCCS, and 8k resistor connect at node A. Both current sources flow into node A. The 8k and 2k resistors connect at node B. The 6mA source, 4k resistor, 2Ix CCCS, and 2k resistor connect at node C. Both current sources flow out of node C.     Because the two current sources are in parallel, they can be combined into an equivalent source. The updated circuit is shown in .   Reduced source transformed circuit.   Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes containing a (6mA + 2Ix) current source, 4k resistor, 8k resistor, and 2k resistor. The current source, 4k resistor, and 8k resistor connect at node A. The current source flows into node A. The 8k and 2k resistors connect at node B. The current source, 4k, and 2k resistors connect at node C. The current source flows out of node C. Ix is defined through Ix and flows from node B to node C. Vth drops over the 2k resistor and drops from node B to node C.     Use the current divider rule to calculate . First, calculate the equivalent resistance of the circuit.     Now can be calculated.     Use Ohm's law to calculate .     Calculate the short circuit current. Because the resistor with the controlling current is shorted, the controlling current is 0 mA (no current will flow through the 2 kΩ resistor when it can travel through a zero-resistance short instead), and the dependent source is effectively deactivated. The circuit diagram depicting this is shown in .   Circuit with load short-circuited.   Circuit with 3 meshes and 2 nodes containing a 6mA source, 4k resistor, 0mA CCCS, and 8k resistor. The 6mA source, 4k resistor, CCCS, and 8k resistor connect at node A. Both current sources flow into node A. All circuit elements also connect at node B. Both current sources flow out of node B. Current Isc is defined through the 8k resistor and flows from node A to node B.     This circuit reduces to the one shown in .   Reduced circuit with load short-circuited.   Circuit with 2 meshes and 2 nodes containing a 6mA source, 4k resistor, and 8k resistor. The 6mA source, 4k resistor, and 8k resistor connect at node A. The current source flows into node A. All circuit elements also connect at node B. The current source flows out of node B. Current Isc is defined through the 8k resistor and flows from node A to node B.     Use a current divider to calculate the short-circuit current.     Use Ohm's law to calculate the Thévenin equivalent resistance.     Therefore, the resistor for maximum power transfer is 4 kΩ. Use to calculate the maximum power transferred to this load.       Given a Norton equivalent circuit, the maximum amount of power will be delivered to the load when the resistance of the load is equal to the Norton equivalent resistance of the circuit. The circuit shown in will be used to demonstrate this theorem.   Circuit diagram used to demonstrate the theorem of maximum power transfer with a Norton equivalent circuit.   Circuit with 2 meshes and 2 nodes containing a In current source, Rn resistor, and Rload resistor. All circuit elements are wired in parallel. All circuit elements connect at node A. The current source flows into node A. All circuit elements connect at node B. The current source flows out of node B. Current I is defined through the Rload resistor and flows from node A to node B.     To determine the value of the resistance of the load ( ) that will lead to maximum power transfer, the power consumed by the load ( ) can be calculated using . This is shown in and derived in .      Ohm's law is used to calculate and the current divider rule is used to calculate .      The derivative of can be taken with respect to . This equation can be set equal to zero to calculate the maximum of the equation. (The full derivation will not be shown in this book.) Maximum power transfer occurs when is equal to .  The maximum power that can be consumed by the load can then be calculated. This value is defined in and derived in .          "
