  "number": "3.8.7",
  "title": "",
  "body": "  Use source transformation to calculate in the circuit shown in (from the superposition section).    3.64 V    It is not useful to convert the 20 V source into a current source. This is because the two current sources would not be in parallel and would not be able to be reduced to an equivalent source. This is shown in the circuit diagram below.   Circuit diagram after converting the voltage source to a current source.   Circuit with 3 meshes and 5 nodes containing a 20mA source, 100ohm resistor, 400ohm resistor, 24mA source, 200ohm resistor, 600ohm resistor, and 700ohm resistor. The 200mA source, 100ohm resistor, 400ohm resistor, and 24mA source connect at node A. Both current sources flow into node A. The 400ohm resistor, 24mA source, and 200ohm resistor connect at node B. The 24mA source flows out of node B. The 200ohm and 600ohm resistors connect at node C. The 600ohm and 700ohm resistors connect at node D. The 200mA source, 100ohm, and 700ohm resistors connect at node E. The 200mA source flows out of node E. Voltage Vx is defined as dropping over the 700ohm resistor from node E to node D.     Instead, convert the 24 mA source to a voltage source.     The circuit is re-drawn below.   Circuit diagram after converting the current source to a voltage source.   Circuit with 1 mesh and 7 nodes containing a 20V source, 100ohm resistor, 9.6V source, 400ohm resistor, 200ohm resistor, 600ohm resistor, and 700ohm resistor. All elements are wired in series. The high-potential end of the 20V source and the 100ohm resistor connect at node A. The 100ohm resistor and high-potential end of the 9.6V source connect at node B. The low-potential end of the 9.6V source and the 400ohm resistor connect at node C. The 400ohm and 200ohm resistors connect at node D. The 200ohm and 600ohm resistors connect at node E. The 600ohm and 700ohm resistors connect at node F. The 700ohm resistor and low-potential end of the 20V source connect at node G. Voltage Vx is defined as dropping over the 700ohm resistor and drops from node G to node F.     The two voltage sources are in series and can be combined into an equivalent source. (Because the 9.6 V source is in the opposite direction as the 20 V source, it will be subtracted.) All of the resistors are also in series and can be combined into an equivalent resistance. (The 700 Ω resistor will not be combined, because the voltage drop over that resistor needs to be calculated.) The reduced circuit is shown below.   Reduced circuit diagram.   Circuit with 1 mesh and 3 nodes containing a 10.4V source, 1300ohm resistor, and 700ohm resistor. All elements are wired in series. The high-potential end of the 10.4V source and 1300ohm resistor connect at node A. The 1300ohm and 700ohm resistors connect at node B. The 700ohm resistor and low-potential end of the 10.4V source connect at node C. Voltage Vx is defined as dropping over the 700ohm resistor from node C to node B.     Use the voltage divider rule to calculate . Note that the polarity of opposes the polarity of the voltage source, which is why the answer is negative.      "
