  "body": "  Use source transformation to calculate in the circuit shown in (from the superposition section).    3.44 mA    It doesn't make sense to convert the 6 mA source into a voltage source, as that voltage source would not be in series with any other voltage source, precluding the possibility of combining sources together to reduce the circuit. This is shown in the circuit diagram below.   Circuit diagram with the 6 mA source transformed into a voltage source.   Circuit with 2 meshes and 6 nodes containing a 108V source, 18k resistor, 12k resistor, 24V source, 5V source, 1k resistor, and 10k resistor. The high-potential end of the 108V source and 18k resistor are connected at node A. The 18k resistor, 12k resistor, and low-potential end of the 5V source connect at node B. The 12k resistor and high-potential end of the 24V source connect at node C. The high-potential end of the 5V source and 1k resistor connect at node D. The 1k and 10k resistors connect at node E. The low-potential end of the 108V source, low-potential end of the 24V source, and 10k resistor connect at node F. Current Ix is defined as flowing through the 10k resistor from node E to node F.     Converting the 5 V source into a current source would similarly be unhelpful. This is shown in the circuit diagram below.   Circuit diagram with the 5 V source transformed into a current source.   Circuit with 4 meshes and 4 nodes containing a 6mA source, 18k resistor, 12k resistor, 24V source, 1k resistor, 5mA source, and 10k resistor. The 6mA source, 18k resistor, 12k resistor, 1k resistor, and 5mA source connect at node A. The 6mA source flows into node A and the 5mA source flows out of node A. The 12k resistor and high-potential end of the 24V source connect at node B. The 1k resistor, 10k resistor, and 5mA source connect at node C. The 5mA source flows into node C. The 6mA source, 18k resistor, low-potential end of the 24V source, and 10k resistor connect at node D. The 6mA source flows out of node D. Current Ix is defined as flowing through the 10k resistor from node C to node D.     Instead, convert the 24 V source into a current source. This transformed current source will be in parallel with the 6 mA source, allowing the circuit to be reduced.     Re-draw the circuit.   Circuit diagram with the 24 V source transformed into a current source.   Circuit with 4 meshes and 4 nodes containing a 6mA source, 18k resistor, 2mA source, 12k resistor, 5V source, 1k resistor, and 10k resistor. The 6mA source, 18k resistor, 2mA source, 12k resistor, and low-potential end of the 5V source connect at node A. The high-potential end of the 5V source and the 1k resistor connect at node B. The 1k and 10k resistors connect at node C. The 6mA source, 18k resistor, 2mA source, 12k resistor, and 10k resistor connect at node D. Both current sources flow out of node D. Current Ix is defined as flowing through the 10k resistor from node C to node D.     The two current sources are in parallel and can be combined into an equivalent source of 8 mA. Furthermore, the 18 kΩ and 12 kΩ resistors are in parallel and can be combined into an equivalent resistance of 7.2 kΩ. The reduced circuit is shown below.   Reduced circuit diagram with the 24 V source transformed into a current source.   Circuit with 2 meshes and 4 nodes containing an 8mA source, 7.2k resistor, 5V source, 1k resistor, and 10k resistor. The 8mA source, 7.2k resistor, and low-potential end of the 5V source connect at node A. The 8mA source flows into node A. The high-potential end of the 5V source and 1k resistor connect at node B. The 1k and 10k resistors connect at node C. The 8mA source, 7.2k resistor, and 10k resistor connect at node D. The 8mA source flows out of node D. Current Ix is defined as flowing through the 10k resistor from node C to node D.     It would still not be helpful to convert the 5 V source into a current source. Instead, convert the 8 mA current source into a voltage source.     The circuit is shown below.   Circuit diagram with the 8 mA source transformed into a voltage source.   Circuit with 1 mesh and 5 nodes containing a 57.6V source. 7.2k resistor, 5V source, 1k resistor, and 10k resistor. All circuit elements are wired in series. The high-potential end of the 57.6V source and 7.2k resistor connect at node A. The 7.2k resistor and low-potential end of the 5V source connect at node B. The high-potential end of the 5V source and 1k resistor connect at node C. The 1k resistor and 10k resistor connect at node D. The 10k resistor and low-potential end of the 57.6V source connect at node E. Current Ix is defined as flowing through the 10k resistor from node D to node E.     The two voltage sources are in series and can be combined into an equivalent voltage source. The three resistors are in series and can be combined into a single equivalent resistance.   Reduced circuit diagram.   Circuit with 1 mesh and 2 nodes containing a 62.6V source and 18.2k resistor. The high-potential end of the 62.6V source and 18.2k resistor connect at node A. The low-potential end of the 62.6V source and 18.2k resistor connect at node B. Current Ix flows through the 18.2k resistor from node A to node B.     Use Ohm's law to calculate .      "
