  "body": "  Calculate the output voltage and circuit gain of the circuit shown in .   Circuit diagram corresponding to .   Op-amp circuit with 0.5V source, 1k resistor, 2k-a resistor, 5k resistor, 2k-b resistor, and 4k resistor. The circuit has 5 nodes. The 1k and 2k-a resistor connect to the inverting input of the op-amp at node A. The 2k-a resistor, 4k resistor, and output of the op-amp connect at node B, labeled Vout. The high-potential end of the 0.5V source connects to the 5k resistor at node C. The 5k resistor, 2k-b resistor, 4k resistor, and non-inverting input of the op-amp connect at node D. The low-potential end of the 0.5V source, 1k resistor, and 2k-b resistor connect to GND at node E.            This circuit doesn't fit any of the standard op-amp circuit templates (inverting, non-inverting, summing, or difference amplifier). Therefore, the assumptions of an op-amp circuit (no input current, virtual node) can be applied with any useful circuit laws and theorems to calculate these parameters.  Apply KCL at the inverting input. The voltage at the inverting input will be referred to as .     Apply KCL at the non-inverting input. The voltage at the non-inverting will be equal to due to the virtual node.     The two equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of , the middle column contains coefficients of , and the right column contains constants.     The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.     is therefore equal to 1.5 V. Use to calculate the circuit gain.      "
