  "type": "Exercise",
  "number": "4.8.8",
  "title": "",
  "body": "  Calculate the value of that should be used in to maximize the power transferred to that load resistor. Then, calculate the power consumed by .   Circuit diagram corresponding to .   Op-amp circuit containing a 9V source, 5k resistor, 8k resistor, 10k resistor, 20k resistor, 4k resistor, and Rload. The circuit has 6 nodes. The 5k resistor, 20k resistor, and inverting input of the op-amp connect at node A. The 20k resistor, 4k resistor, and output of the op-amp connect at node B. The 4k resistor and Rload connect at node C, labeled Vout. The high-potential end of the 9V source connects to the 8k resistor at node D. The 8k resistor, 10k resistor, and non-inverting input of the op-amp connect at node E. The low-potential end of the 9V source, 10k resistor, and Rload connect to GND at node F.            Calculate and to calculate , which is the resistance used to obtain the maximum power transferred to the load. The circuit is a non-inverting op-amp. Use the voltage divider rule to calculate .     Use to calculate the gain of the op-amp circuit.     is equal to the output voltage of the circuit. (Because the output is an open-circuit, no current flows through the 4 kΩ resistor, and it does not contribute to the circuit.) Use to calculate this voltage.     The short-circuit current is equal to the current between the output of the op-amp and ground when the load resistor is shorted. The output voltage of the op-amp will be 5 V and the other end of the 4 kΩ resistor will be grounded. This leads to a short-circuit current of     The Thévenin equivalent resistance can now be calculated. This is equal to the load resistance that should be used to maximize the power transferred to the load.     Use to calculate the maximum power transferred to the load.      "
