  "number": "4.8.10",
  "title": "",
  "body": "  Calculate the output voltage and source gains of the circuit shown in .   Circuit diagram corresponding to .   Op-amp circuit with 2 op-amps, 0.5V source (V1), 1V source (V2), four 2k resistors, one 4k resistor, and two 3k resistors. The high-potential end of V1 connects to 2k-a at node A. The 2k-a resistor, 4k resistor, and 2k-b resistor connect to the inverting input of op-amp 1 at node B. The 4k resistor, 2k-d resistor, and output of op-amp 1 connect at node C. The 2k-d resistor, 3k-a resistor, and inverting input of op-amp 2 connect at node D. The 2k-b resistor, 3k-a resistor, 3k-b resistor, and output of op-amp 2 connect at node E, labeled Vout. The high-potential end of V2 connects to the non-inverting input of op-amp 2 at node F. The 2k-c resistor, 3k-b resistor, and non-inverting input of op-amp 1 connect at node G. The low-potential end of both sources and 2k-c resistor connect to GND at node H.            This circuit doesn't fit any of the standard op-amp circuit templates (inverting, non-inverting, summing, or difference amplifier). Therefore, the assumptions of an op-amp circuit (no input current, virtual node) can be applied with any useful circuit laws and theorems to calculate these parameters. Because there is a connection between the input of the left op-amp and the output of the right op-amp, cascading op-amp properties cannot be used to calculate the overall circuit gain.  This solution will use superposition to solve the circuit. Adding the two subcircuit values of will yield the overall value of , and each subcircuit can be used to calculate the individual source gain.  Deactivate source and draw the circuit diagram to calculate and .   Circuit diagram with deactivated.   Op-amp circuit with 2 op-amps, a 0.5V source (V1), four 2k resistors, two 3k resistors, and 4k resistor. The circuit has 7 nodes. The high-potential end of V1 connects to the 2k-a resistor at node A. The 2k-a resistor, 2k-b resistor, 4k resistor, and inverting input of op-amp 1 connect at node B. The 4k resistor, 2k-d resistor, and output of op-amp 1 connect at node C. The 2k-d resistor, 3k-a resistor, and inverting input of op-amp 2 connect at node D. The 2k-b resistor, 3k-a resistor, 3k-b resistor, and output of op-amp 2 connect at node E, labeled Vout,1. The 2k-c resistor, 3k-b resistor, and non-inverting input to op-amp 1 connect at node F. The low-potential end of V1, 2k-c resistor, and non-inverting input of op-amp 2 connect to GND at node G.     Perform KCL at the inverting input of the left op-amp. This node voltage will be referred to as . The left op-amp's output voltage will be referred to as .     Perform KCL at the non-inverting input of the left op-amp.     Perform KCL at the inverting input of the right op-amp.     The three equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of , the middle-left column contains coefficients of , the middle-right column contains coefficients of , and the right column contains constants.     The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.     is therefore equal to 1.5 V. The source gain from source can now be calculated.     Deactivate source and draw the circuit diagram to calculate and .   Circuit diagram with deactivated.   Op-amp circuit with 2 op-amps, 1V source (V2), four 2k resistors, two 3k resistors, and 4k resistor. The circuit has 7 nodes. The 2k-a resistor, 2k-b resistor, 4k resistor, and inverting input to op-amp 1 connect at node A. The 4k resistor, 2k-d resistor, and output of op-amp 2 connect at node B. The 2k-d resistor, 3k-a resistor, and inverting input of op-amp 2 connect at node C. The 2k-b resistor, 3k-a resistor, 3k-b resistor, and output of op-amp 2 connect at node D, labeled Vout,2. The high-potential end of V2 connects to the non-inverting input of op-amp 2 at node E. The 2k-c resistor, 3k-b resistor, and non-inverting input to op-amp 1 connect at node F. The 2k-a resistor, 2k-c resistor, and low-potential end of V2 connect to GND at node G.     Perform KCL at the inverting input of the left op-amp. This node voltage will be referred to as . The left op-amp's output voltage will be referred to as .     Perform KCL at the non-inverting input of the left op-amp.     Perform KCL at the inverting input of the right op-amp.     The three equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of , the middle-left column contains coefficients of , the middle-right column contains coefficients of , and the right column contains constants.     The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.     is therefore equal to 2.5 V. The source gain from source can now be calculated.     Use superposition to calculate .      "
