  "body": " Charging transient response   Calculate and for the circuit shown in . The switch is open for a very long time before closing at a time of 0 seconds.   A non-homogeneous RC circuit.   Circuit with 3 meshes and 5 nodes consisting of an 800uA source, 40k resistor, 10k resistor, 30k resistor, 5k resistor, 20nF capacitor, and SPST switch. The 800uA source, 40k, and SPST switch connect at node a. The 800uA source flows into node a. The other end of the SPST switch and 10k connect at node b. The 10k, 30k, and 5k connect at node c. The 5k and capacitor connect at node d. The 800uA source, 40k, 30k, and capacitor connect at node e. Voltage v(t) is defined over the capacitor from node d to node e. Branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor from node d to node e. The SPST is depicted as closing.     In the initial steady-state, the capacitor is disconnected from any source and has fully discharged. Therefore, .  The final steady-state conditions can be calculated. Re-draw the circuit to represent the time after the switch closes. Because the circuit is under DC conditions, the capacitor can be represented as an open.     Circuit with 2 meshes and 5 nodes consisting of an 800uA source, 40k resistor, 10k resistor, 30k resistor, 5k resistor, and SPST switch. The 800uA source, 40k, and SPST switch connect at node a. The 800uA source flows into node a. The other end of the SPST switch and 10k connect at node b. The 10k, 30k, and 5k connect at node c. The 5k connects at node d. The 800uA source, 40k, and 30k connect at node e. Voltage v(infinity) is defined from node d to node e. The SPST is depicted as closed.     The tool that will be used to solve for in this example is source transformation. Source transformation will enable us to calculate using the voltage divider rule. Transform the 800 µA source to a voltage source and re-draw the circuit diagram.        Circuit with 2 meshes and 5 nodes consisting of a 32V source, 40k resistor, 10k resistor, 30k resistor, and 5k resistor. The high-potential end of the 32V source and 40k connect at node a. The 40k and 10k connect at node b. The 10k, 30k, and 5k connect at node c. The 5k connects at node d. The low-potential end of the 32V source, and 30k connect at node e. Voltage v(infinity) is defined from node d to node e.     Use the voltage divider rule to calculate . Note that the 5 kΩ resistor does not contribute to as no current flows through it in a steady-state condition.     Analyze the charging circuit. Re-draw for time .     Circuit with 3 meshes and 5 nodes consisting of an 800uA source, 40k resistor, 10k resistor, 30k resistor, 5k resistor, 20nF capacitor, and SPST switch. The 800uA source, 40k, and SPST switch connect at node a. The 800uA source flows into node a. The other end of the SPST switch and 10k connect at node b. The 10k, 30k, and 5k connect at node c. The 5k and capacitor connect at node d. The 800uA source, 40k, 30k, and capacitor connect at node e. Voltage v(t) is defined over the capacitor from node d to node e. Branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor from node d to node e. The SPST is depicted as closed.     Deactivate the current source to calculate the equivalent resistance seen by the capacitor.     Circuit with 2 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of a 40k resistor, 10k resistor, 30k resistor, 5k resistor, and 20nF capacitor. The 40k and 10k connect at node a. The 10k, 30k, and 5k connect at node b. The 5k and capacitor connect at node c. The 40k, 30k, and capacitor connect at node d. Voltage v(t) is defined over the capacitor from node c to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor from node c to node d.     The equivalent resistance is calculated below.     Use to calculate .     The equations for and can now be derived using and .     These two signals are plotted below as functions of time.     Top: v(t) in V plotted as a function of time in ms. Function starts at 0V until 0ms, then exponentially increases to 8V over time. Bottom: i(t) in mA plotted as a function of time in ms. Function is 0 until 0-, then jumps to 0.51mA at 0+, and exponentially decays to 0 over time.      "
