  "body": "  Calculate an expression for and given the circuit shown in . The switch is open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds.   Circuit diagram corresponding to .   Circuit with 6 nodes consisting of a 65V source, SPST switch, 600uF cap, and resistors of 30ohm, 20ohm, 40ohm, and 100ohm. The high-potential end of the 65V source connects to 30ohm at node a. The 30ohm resistor connects to one end of the SPST switch at node b. The other end of the SPST switch connects to the 20ohm at node c. The 20ohm, 600uF, and 40ohm connect at node d. The 40ohm and 100ohm connect at node e. The low-potential end of the 65V source, 600uF, and 100ohm connect at node f. Voltage v(t) drops over the 100ohm resistor from node e to node f. Branch current i(t) flows through the 100ohm resistor from node e to node f. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.            The capacitor has an initial voltage of 0 V because it is not connected to the source. This will be true of the 100 Ω resistor as well. However, the voltage over a resistor may not be continuous with time. Analyze the circuit at to confirm the value of . Treat the capacitor as a source providing 0 V to the circuit.     Circuit with 2 meshes and 5 nodes consisting of a 65V source, 0V source, 30ohm, 20ohm, 40ohm, and 100ohm resistors. The high-potential end of the 65V source connects to 30ohm at node a. The 30ohm and 20ohm connect at node b. The 20ohm, 40ohm, and high-potential end of the 0V source connect at node c. The 40ohm and 100ohm connect at node d. The low-potential end of both sources and the 100ohm connect at node e. Voltage v(0+) drops over the 100ohm resistor from node e to node f. Branch current i(0+) flows through the 100ohm resistor from node e to node f.     The value of will also be 0 V (confirm with the voltage divider rule if it's not obvious from observing the circuit diagram).  Analyze the circuit in its final steady-state configuration to calculate . Treat the capacitor as an open.     Circuit with 1 mesh and 5 nodes consisting of a 65V source and resistors of 30ohms, 20ohms, 40ohms, and 100ohms. The high-potential end of the 65V source connects to 30ohm at node a. The 30ohm and 20ohm connect at node b. The 20ohm and 40ohm connect at node c. The 40ohm and 100ohm connect at node d. The low-potential end of the 65V source connects to the 100ohm at node e. Voltage v(infinity) drops over the 100ohm resistor from node d to node e. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the 100ohm resistor from node d to node e.     All of the resistors are in series, therefore, the voltage divider rule can be used to calculate .     Analyze the circuit at to calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor. (The capacitor will not be treated as a source at this point, as the goal is to calculate equivalent resistance and not voltage.) Deactivate the voltage source by converting it to a short.     Circuit with 2 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of a 30ohm, 20ohm, 40ohm, and 100ohm resistors and a 600uF capacitor. The 30ohm and 20ohm connect at node a. The 20ohm, 600uF, and 40ohm connect at node b. The 40ohm and 100ohm connect at node c. The 30ohm, 600uF, and 100ohm connect at node d.     Combine both sets of series resistors and re-draw the circuit.     Circuit with 2 meshes and 2 nodes consisting of a 50ohm resistor, 140ohm resistor, and 600uF capacitor. All three circuit elements are connected in parallel.     The two resistors are in parallel.     Use to calculate .     Use to derive an equation for .     Because the voltage and current are measured over a resistor, Ohm's law will be used to calculate .      "
