  "title": "Charging from zero transient response.",
  "body": " Charging from zero transient response   Calculate and for the circuit below. The switch is open for a very long time before closing at a time of 0 seconds.     Circuit with 4 nodes consisting of a 6mA source, three resistors (two 10ohm and one 12ohm), a 50mH inductor, and an SPST switch. The 6mA source connects to both 10ohm resistors at node a. The 6mA source flows into node a. The 10ohm (B) connects to one end of the SPST switch at node b. The other end of the SPST switch connects to the 12ohm and 50mH at node c. The 6mA source, 10ohm (A), 50mH, and 12ohm connect at node d. The 6mA source flows out of node d. Voltage v(t) drops over the inductor from node c to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node c to node d. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.     The initial voltage and current properties of the inductor are zero. This is because the inductor is disconnected from the source in the initial steady-state, and any current has long since discharged through the 12 Ω resistor.  Calculate the final (steady-state) conditions of the circuit. Re-draw the circuit to represent the time well after the switch closes. Because the circuit is under DC conditions, the inductor can be represented as a short.     Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of a 6mA source, a short, two 10ohm resistors, and a 12ohm resistor. The 6mA source connects to both 10ohm resistors at node a. The 6mA source flows into node a. The 10ohm resistor (B), one end of the short, and the 12ohm connect at node b. The 6mA source, 12ohm, 10ohm (A), and the other end of the short connect at node c. The 6mA source flows out of node c. Voltage v(infinity) is defined over the short from node b to node c. Branch current i(infinity) is defined through the short from node b to node c.     The 12 Ω resistor is shorted out of the circuit by the inductor. Re-draw the circuit with that resistor removed.     Circuit with 2 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of a 6mA source and two 10ohm resistors. All three circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. The 6mA source flows from node a to node b. Branch current i(infinity) flows through one of the 10ohm resistors from node a to node b.     Use the current divider rule to calculate . The equivalent resistance of the circuit is 5 Ω.     Analyze the charging circuit. Re-draw for time , which is when the switch is closed. Deactivate the current source by replacing it with an open to calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor.     Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of two 10ohm resistors, a 12ohm resistor, and a 50mH inductor. The two 10ohm resistors connect at node a. The 10ohm (B), 50mH, and 12ohm connect at node b. The 10ohm (A), 50mH, and 12ohm connect at node c. Voltage v(t) is defined over the inductor from node b to node c. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node b to node c.     The equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor is calculated below.     Use to calculate the RL time constant.     The equations for and can now be derived using and .     These two signals are plotted below as functions of time.     Top: Graph of i(t) in mA plotted as a function of t in ms. The graph is at 0mA until a time of 0, then exponentially decays to 3mA. Bottom: graph of v(t) in V plotted on the same x-axis. The graph is 0 until 0+, where it jumps to (0,22.5), then exponentially decays to 0.      "
