  "title": "Charging from zero transient response with a dependent source.",
  "body": " Charging from zero transient response with a dependent source   Calculate and for the circuit shown below. Assume that the inductor is completely discharged in the initial steady-state condition. The switch has been open for a very long time before closing at a time of 0 seconds. The parameter values are , , , , and .     Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of a current source Is, resistors R1 and R2, VCVS k*Vx, inductor L, and SPST switch. Is, R1, and the high-potential end of the VCVS connect at node a. Is flows into node a. The low-potential end of the VCVS connects to R2 at node b. R2 connects to one end of the SPST switch at node c. The other end of the SPST switch connects to L at node d. Is, R1, and L connect at node e. Is flows out of node e. Voltage Vx is defined over R1 from node a to node e. Voltage v(t) is defined over L from node d to node e. Branch current i(t) is defined through L and flows from node d to node e. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.     Analyze the circuit in the final steady-state condition. In this condition, the switch has been closed for a long time. The inductor can be replaced by a short. Numeric quantities for each component will be used in this circuit diagram.     Circuit with 2A source, 2ohm resistor, 3ohm resistor, and 0.1*Vx VCVS. The 2A source, 2ohm, and high-potential end of the VCVS connect at node a. The 2A source flows into node a. The low-potential end of the VCVS connects to the 3ohm at node b. The 3ohm, 2ohm, and 2A source connect at node c. The 2A source flows out of node c. Vx drops over the 2ohm resistor from node a to node c. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the 3ohm resistor from node b to node c.     Source transformation can be used in this circuit, but it is not terribly intuitive, as it changes the position of in the circuit. Instead, KCL and KVL will be used.  First, use KCL at the node connecting all three circuit branches.     Then, use KCL around the right mesh of the circuit.      Method 1: Thévenin equivalent circuit   There are two equations and two unknowns. These two equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of , the middle column contains coefficients of , and the right column contains constants. The units of V, A, and Ω are used.     The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.     This means that .  There are two methods that can be used to calculate . In either case, the circuit should be analyzed at , so the switch will be closed. The first method that will be used in this example is to calculate the Thévenin equivalent circuit as seen by the inductor and use that Thévenin equivalent resistance in .  The Thévenin equivalent circuit is shown below.     Circuit with 4 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of current source Is, resistors R1 and R2, and k*Vx VCVS. Is, R1, and the high-potential end of the VCVS connect at node a. Is flows into node a. The low-potential end of the VCVS connects to R2 at node b. R2 connects at node c. Is and R1 connect at node d. Is flows out of node d. Vx drops over R1 from node a to node d. The Thevenin equivalent circuit is measured from node c to node d.     Use the test source method to derive the Thévenin equivalent resistance. The independent current source has been deactivated.     Circuit with 4 nodes and one mesh consisting of voltage source Vtest, VCVS k*Vx, and resistors R1 and R2. R1 and the high-potential end of the VCVS connect at node a. The low-potential end of the VCVS connects to R2 at node b. R2 connects to the high-potential end of Vtest at node c. The low-potential end of Vtest connects to R1 at node d. Voltage Vx drops over R1 from node a to node d. Branch current Itest is defined as flowing through Vtest from node do to node c.     Use Ohm's law over to derive an equation for .     Use KCL around the circuit's single loop to derive an equation for .     Plug into .     Use Ohm's law to calculate the Thévenin equivalent resistance.     Use to calculate .      Method 2: Deriving the differential equation   The second method that will be used is to derive the differential equation and use to calculate .     Circuit with 4 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of current source Is, VCVS k*Vx, resistors R1 and R2, and inductor L. Is, R1, and the high-potential end of the VCVS connect at node a. Is flows into node a. The low-potential end of the VCVS connects to R2 at node b. R2 and L connect at node c. Is, R1, and L connect at node d. Is flows out of node d. Voltage Vx drops over R1 from node a to node d. Voltage v(t) drops over L from node c to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node c to node d.     Use KCL around the right loop of the circuit.     Use KCL at the node connecting the three circuit branches.     Solve for .     Plug into and reduce to standard form.     Numeric quantities for each parameter can now be plugged in to calculate the differential equation. Units are not included in the equation, but base units of A, Ω, and H are used.     This yields the same result as the Thévenin equivalent circuit method, as expected. If we had not already derived the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor, we could use to solve for .     Use to derive an equation for and to derive an equation for .      "
