  "title": "General transient response of an inductor.",
  "body": " General transient response of an inductor   Calculate for the circuit below. Switch S1 is closed for a very long time before opening at a time of 0 seconds. Switch S2 is open for a very long time before closing at a time of 0.005 seconds.     Circuit with 6 nodes consisting of an 8V source, 12V source, four 12ohm resistors (R1, R2, R3, and R4), a 20mH inductor, and two SPST switches (S1 and S2). The high-potential end of the 8V source connects to R1 at node a. R1 connects to one end of S1 at node b. The other end of S1 connects to R2, S2, and the 20mH inductor at node c. The other end of S2, R3, and R4 connect at node d. R4 connects to the high-potential end of the 12V source at node e. The low-potential end of both sources, R2, R3, and the 20mH inductor connect at node f. Branch current i(t) is defined as flowing through the 20mH inductor from node c to node f. Switch S1 is depicted as opening. Switch S2 is depicted as closing.     Analyze the initial conditions of the circuit. Switch S1 has been closed for a long time and switch S2 is open. The inductor can be replaced with a short.     Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of an 8V source, two 12ohm resistors (R1 and R2) and a short. The high-potential end of the 8V source connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, and the short connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 8V source, R2, and the short connect at node c. Branch current i(0) is defined as flowing through the short from node b to node c.     The 12 Ω resistor in parallel with the inductor (which acts like a short) is shorted out of the circuit and can be removed.     Circuit with 2 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of an 8V source and 12ohm resistor. The high-potential end of the 8V source connects to the 12ohm resistor at node a. The 12ohm resistor connects to the low-potential end of the 8V source at node b. Branch current i(0) flows through the 12ohm resistor from node a to node b.     Use Ohm's law to calculate .     This means the equation for will contain a term of , representing the initial steady-state conditions of the circuit. This creates an graph that so far appears as follows.     Graph of i(t) in mA plotted as function of time in ms. The graph is a straight line from (-5,667) to (0,667).     Analyze the circuit with both switches open to determine how the circuit will act between 0 s and 0.005 s (5 ms). Both sources are disconnected from the circuit in this interval of time.     Circuit with 2 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of a 12ohm resistor and 20mH inductor. The two circuit elements connect on both ends at each node. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor.     The equivalent resistance seen by the inductor is 12 Ω during this time interval. Use to calculate during this interval of time.     This means that the equation for will contain a term of . The graph of the current containing both terms appears below.     Graph of i(t) in mA plotted as function of time in ms. The graph is a straight line from (-5,667) to (0,667). Then the current exponentially decays to zero.     Before analyzing the circuit when S2 closes, calculate the value of at 5 ms, which represents the initial condition experienced by the inductor when that switch closes.     At 0.005 s, the exponential term describing the decay with no longer dictates how the circuit operates. This term must be subtracted out at . Therefore, the equation for will contain a term of . The graph of the current containing all three terms is shown below.     Graph of i(t) in mA plotted as function of time in ms. The graph is a straight line from (-5,667) to (0,667). Then the current exponentially decays to (5,33.2).     Analyze the circuit at to calculate the equivalent resistance seen by the inductor to calculate the value of in this condition. The 12 V source has been deactivated.     Circuit with 2 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of three 12ohm resistors and a 20mH inductor. All four circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node a to node b.     The three 12 Ω resistors are in parallel. Therefore, the equivalent resistance is one-third of 12 Ω, which is 4 Ω. Use to calculate the value of for the circuit after 0.005 s.     Analyze the circuit in the final steady-state condition to calculate . Switch S1 is open and S2 has been closed for a very long time. The inductor can be replaced by a short.     Circuit with 3 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of three 12ohm resistors (R1, R2, and R3), a short, and a 12V source. R1, R2, R3, and the short all connect at node a. R3 and the high-potential end of the 12V source connect at node b. R1, R2, the short, and the low-potential end of the 12V source connect at node c. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the short from node a to node c.     The two resistors in parallel with the inductor (which acts like a short) are shorted out of the circuit and can be removed.     Circuit with 2 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of a 12ohm resistor and 12V source. The 12ohm resistor connects to the low-potential end of the 12V source at node a. The high-potential end of the 12V source connects to the 12ohm resistor at node b. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the 12ohm resistor from node b to node a.     Use Ohm's law to calculate .     This means the equation for will contain a term of . The graph of including all terms is shown below.     Graph of i(t) in mA plotted as function of time in ms. The graph is a straight line from (-5,667) to (0,667). Then the current exponentially decays to (5,33.2). Then the current exponentially decays to 1000 with a different time-constant.     The overall equation for is expressed below.      "
