  "number": "6.6.14",
  "title": "",
  "body": "  Derive equations for and for the circuit shown in . The switch has been closed for a very long time before opening at a time of zero seconds.   Circuit diagram corresponding to .   Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of a 1.8V source, three resistors, one inductor, and an SPST switch. The high-potential end of the 1.8V source connects to the 30ohm resistor at node a. The 30ohm resistor connects to one end of the SPST switch at node b. The other end of the SPST switch, 20ohm and 40ohm resistors connect at node c. The 40ohm resistor connects to the 800mH inductor at node d. The 800mH inductor, 20ohm resistor, and low-potential end of the 1.8V source connect at node e. Voltage v(t) drops over the 20ohm resistor from node c to node e. Branch current i(t) flows through the 20ohm resistor from node c to node e. Branch current iL(t) flows through the inductor from node d to node e. The SPST switch is depicted as opening.            Analyze the circuit in the initial steady-state condition. The inductor can be replaced by a short. Because the current through the resistor may be discontinuous with time, both and should be calculated.     Circuit with 2 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of a 1.8V source and three resistors. The high-potential end of the 1.8V source connects to the 30ohm resistor at node a. The 30ohm, 20ohm, and 40ohm resistors connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 1.8V source connects to the 20ohm and 40ohm resistors at node c. Voltage v(0-) drops over the 20ohm resistor from node b to node c. Branch current i(0-) flows through the 20ohm resistor from node b to node c. Branch current iL(0) flows through the 40ohm resistor from node b to node c.     Perform source transformation to convert the voltage source into a current source.        Circuit with 2 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of a 60mA source, 30ohm resistor, 20ohm resistor, and 40ohm resistor. All four circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. The 60mA source flows out of node b and into node a. Voltage v(0-) drops over the 20ohm resistor from node a to node b. Branch current i(0-) flows through the 20ohm resistor from node a to node b. Branch current iL(0) flows through the 40ohm resistor from node a to node b.     Calculate the equivalent resistance of the circuit.     Use the current divider rule to calculate .     Use the current divider rule to calculate .     Analyze the circuit at to calculate . The inductor can be treated as a current source with a value of . The voltage source and 30 Ω resistor are disconnected from the circuit at this time.     Circuit with 3 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of a 13.8mA source and two resistors. The 20ohm and 40ohm resistors connect at node a. The 40ohm resistor and 13.8mA source connect at node b. The 13.8mA source flows out of node b. The 13.8mA source connects to the 20ohm resistor at node c. The 13.8mA source flows into node c. Branch current i(0+) flows through the 20ohm resistor from node a to node c.     Due to KCL, must be equal to 13.8 mA.  Analyze the circuit at to calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor. The voltage source and 30 Ω resistor are disconnected from the circuit at this time.     Circuit with 3 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of a 20ohm resistor, 40ohm resistor, and 800mH inductor, all wired in series.     The equivalent resistance is equal to the series combination of both resistors, which is 60 Ω. Use to calculate .     Use to derive an equation for .     Use Ohm's law to derive an equation for .      "
