  "title": "",
  "body": "  Derive equations for and for the circuit shown in . The switch has been closed for a very long time before opening at a time of zero seconds. The parameter values are , , , and .   Circuit diagram corresponding to .   Circuit with 6 nodes consisting of a 0.4V source, five resistors, inductor, SPST switch, and CCVS rm*iX(t). The high-potential end of the 0.4V source connects to the 1ohm resistor at node a. The 1ohm, 2ohm, and 6ohm resistors connect at node b. The 6ohm resistor connects to one end of the SPST switch at node c. The other end of the SPST switch, inductor L, and high-potential end of the CCVS connect at node d. The low-potential end of the CCVS connects to R1 and R2 at node e. The low-potential end of the 0.4V source, 2ohm resistor, L, R1, and R2 connect at node f. Voltage v(t) drops over the inductor from node d to node f. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node d to node f. Branch current iX(t) flows through R1 from node e to node f. The SPST switch is depicted as opening.            Analyze the circuit in the initial steady-state condition. The inductor can be replaced by a short.     Circuit with 5 nodes and 4 meshes consisting of a 0.4V source, five resistors, rm*iX(0) CCVS, and a short. The high-potential end of the 0.4V source connects to the 1ohm resistor at node a. The 1ohm, 2ohm, and 6ohm resistors connect at node b. The 6ohm resistor, high-potential end of the CCVS, and short connect at node c. The low-potential end of the CCVS connects to R1 and R2 at node d. The low-potential end of the 0.4V source, 2ohm resistor, short, and R1 and R2 connect at node e. Branch current i(0) flows through the short from node c to node e. Branch current iX(0) flows through R1 from node d to node e.     The CCVS and resistors and are shorted out of the circuit and can be removed.     Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of a 0.4V source and three resistors. The high-potential end of the 0.4V source connects to the 1ohm resistor at node a. The 1ohm, 2ohm, and 6ohm resistors connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 0.4V source connects to the 2ohm and 6ohm resistors at node c. Branch current i(0) flows through the 6ohm resistor from node b to node c.     Perform source transformation to convert the voltage source into a current source.        Circuit with 2 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of a 0.4A source, 1ohm resistor, 2ohm resistor, and 6ohm resistor. All four circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. The current source flows out of node b into node a. Branch current i(0) flows through the 6ohm resistor from node a to node b.     Calculate the equivalent resistance of the circuit.     Use the current divider rule to calculate .     Analyze the circuit at . The voltage source, 1 Ω resistor, 2 Ω resistor, and 6 Ω resistor are disconnected from the circuit.     Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of an inductor L, CCVS rm*iX(t), and two resistors R1 and R2. L and the high-potential end of the CCVS connect at node a. The low-potential end of the CCVS connects to R1 and R2 at node b. L, R1, and R2 connect at node c. Voltage v(t) drops over the inductor from node a to node c. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node a to node c. Branch current iX(t) flows through R1 from node b to node c.     There are two methods that can be used to calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor. The first method that will be used is to derive the Thévenin equivalent circuit as seen by the inductor.     Circuit with 3 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of a CCVS (rm*iX(t)), and resistors R1 and R2. The high-potential end of the CCVS connects at node a. The low-potential end of the CCVS connects to R1 and R2 at node c. R1 and R2 connect at node b. Branch current iX(t) flows through R1 from node c to node b. The Thevenin equivalent circuit is defined between nodes a and b.     Use the test source method to calculate the Thévenin equivalent resistance.     Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of a voltage source Vtest, CCVS (rm*iX(t)), and resistors R1 and R2. The high-potential end of Vtest and the CCVS connect at node a. The low-potential end of the CCVS connects to R1 and R2 at node c. The low-potential end of Vtest, R1, and R2 connect at node b. Branch current iX(t) flows through R1 from node c to node b. Branch current Itest flows through Vtest from node b to node a.     Use KVL around the left-hand mesh to derive an equation for .     Use KCL at the node connecting all three circuit branches to derive an equation for . (Note that the voltage dropped over is equal to the voltage dropped over , which is equal to .)     Use Ohm's law to calculate the Thévenin equivalent resistance.     Plug in numerical quantities to derive this value.     Use to calculate .     The second method that can be used to solve the circuit is to derive a differential equation.     Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of an inductor L, CCVS rm*iX(t), and two resistors R1 and R2. L and the high-potential end of the CCVS connect at node a. The low-potential end of the CCVS connects to R1 and R2 at node b. L, R1, and R2 connect at node c. Voltage v(t) drops over the inductor from node a to node c. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node a to node c. Branch current iX(t) flows through R1 from node b to node c.     Use KVL around the left-hand mesh.     Use KCL at the node connecting all three circuit branches. (Note that the voltage dropped over is equal to the voltage dropped over , which is equal to .)     Solve for and simplify.     Plug the simplified equation for into and reduce to standard form.     Plug in numerical quantities to obtain the differential equation.     This yields the same result as the Thévenin equivalent circuit method, as expected. If we had not already derived the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor, we could use to solve for .  Use to derive an equation for .     Use to derive an equation for .      "
