  "body": "  Derive equations for and given the circuit shown in . The switch has been in position b for a very long time before moving to position a at a time of zero seconds.   Circuit diagram corresponding to .   Circuit with 6 nodes consisting of a 2.7V source, four resistors, one inductor, and SPDT switch. The 78ohm resistor connects to output a of the SPDT switch at node a. Output b of the SPDT switch connects to the 52ohm resistor at node b. The input of the SPDT switch connects to the 760mH inductor at node c. The 52ohm, 24ohm, and 7ohm resistors connect at node d. The low-potential end of the 2.7V source connects to the 760mH inductor and 24ohm and 7ohm resistors at node e. The high-potential end of the 2.7V source connects to the 78ohm resistor at node f. Voltage v(t) drops over the inductor from node c to node e. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node c to node e. The SPDT switch is depicted as switching from position b to position a.            Analyze the circuit in the final steady-state condition to calculate . The inductor can be replaced by a short.     Circuit with 2 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of a 2.7V source in series with a 78ohm resistor. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the circuit from the high-potential end of the source to the low-potential end of the source.     Use Ohm's law to calculate .     Analyze the circuit at to calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor.     Circuit with 3 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of a 2.7V source, 78ohm resistor, and 760mH inductor, all connected in series.     Deactivate the voltage source.     Circuit with 2 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of a 78ohm resistor and 760mH inductor wired in series.     The equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor is 78 Ω. Use to calculate .     Use to derive an equation for .     Use to derive an equation for .      "
