  "body": "  Derive equations for and given the circuit shown in . The switch has been open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds.   Circuit diagram corresponding to .   Circuit with 6 nodes consisting of a 10V source, four resistors, inductor, and SPST switch. The high-potential end of the 10V source connects to the 200ohm resistor at node a. The 200ohm resistor connects to one end of the SPST switch at node b. The other end of the SPST switch connects to the 250mH inductor and 500ohm (A) resistor at node c. The 500ohm (A) and 500ohm (B) resistors connect at node d. The 250mH inductor connects to the 250ohm resistor at node e. The low-potential end of the 10V source connects to the 250ohm resistor and 500ohm (B) resistor at node f. Voltage v(t) drops over the 250ohm resistor from node e to node f. Branch current i(t) flows through the 250ohm resistor from node e to node f. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.            First, simplify the circuit by combining the two series resistors.     Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of a 10V source, three resistors, inductor, and SPST switch. The high-potential end of the 10V source connects to the 200ohm resistor at node a. The 200ohm resistor connects to one end of the SPST switch at node b. The other end of the SPST switch connects to the 250mH inductor and 1000ohm resistor at node c. The 250mH inductor connects to the 250ohm resistor at node d. The low-potential end of the 10V source connects to the 250ohm resistor and 1000ohm resistor at node e. Voltage v(t) drops over the 250ohm resistor from node d to node e. Branch current i(t) flows through the 250ohm resistor from node d to node e. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.     Analyze the circuit in the final steady-state condition. The inductor can be replaced by a short.     Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of a 10V source and three resistors. The high-potential end of the 10V source connects to the 200ohm resistor at node a. The 200ohm, 250ohm, and 1000ohm resistors connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 10V source connects to the 250ohm and 1000ohm resistors at node c. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the 250ohm resistor from node b to node c.     Use source transformation to convert the voltage source into a current source.        Circuit with 2 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of a 50mA source, 200ohm resistor, 250ohm resistor, and 1000ohm resistor. All four circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. The 50mA source flows out of node b into node a. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the 250ohm resistor from node a to node b.     Calculate the equivalent resistance of the circuit.     The voltage is dropped in parallel with all three resistors. Use Ohm's law with the source current and to calculate this value.     Use Ohm's law through the branch with the 250 Ω resistor to calculate .     Analyze the circuit at to calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor.     Circuit with 4 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of a 10V source, three resistors, and one inductor. The high-potential end of the 10V source connects to the 200ohm resistor at node a. The 200ohm resistor, 250mH inductor, and 1000ohm resistor connect at node b. The 250mH inductor connects to the 250ohm resistor at node c. The low-potential end of the 10V source connects to the 250ohm and 1000ohm resistors at node d. Voltage v(t) drops over the 250ohm resistor from node c to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through the 250ohm resistor from node c to node d.     Deactivate the voltage source.     Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of three resistors and one inductor. The 200ohm resistor, 250mH inductor, and 1000ohm resistor connect at node a. The 250mH inductor connects to the 250ohm resistor at node b. The 200ohm, 250ohm, and 1000ohm resistors at node c. Voltage v(t) drops over the 250ohm resistor from node b to node c. Branch current i(t) flows through the 250ohm resistor from node b to node c.     Calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor.     Use to calculate .     Use to derive an equation for .     Use Ohm's law to calculate .      "
