  "body": "  Derive an equation for given the circuit shown in . The switch is in position a for a very long time before moving to position b at a time of zero seconds. Assume the op-amp is sufficiently sourced such that the output will not saturate. The parameter values are , , and . The inductor has a parasitic resistance of 70 Ω.   Circuit diagram corresponding to .   Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of an op-amp, voltage source Vs, SPDT switch, inductor L, and resistor RF. Output a of the SPDT switch connects to ground at node a. Output b of the SPDT switch connects to the high-potential end of Vs at node b. The-low potential end of Vs connects to ground (node a). The input of the SPDT switch connects to the non-inverting input of the op-amp at node c. L, RF, and the inverting input of the op-amp connect at node d. The other end of L connects to ground (node a). The other end of RF connects to the output of the op-amp at node e. Voltage v(t) is measured between node e and ground.            Analyze the circuit in the final steady-state condition. The inductor can be replaced by its parasitic resistance.     Circuit with 4 nodes consisting of an op-amp, voltage source Vs and resistors RP and RF. The high-potential end of Vs connects to the non-inverting input of the op-amp at node a. The low-potential end of Vs connects to ground (node d). RP, RF, and the inverting input of the op-amp connect at node b. The other end of RP connects to ground (node d). The other end of RF connects to the output of the op-amp at node c. Voltage v(t) is measured between node c and ground.     This is a non-inverting op-amp circuit. Use to calculate the gain of the op-amp.     Use to calculate .     Analyze the circuit at . The parasitic resistance will be explicitly drawn in this circuit to make it clear.     Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of an op-amp, voltage source Vs, inductor L, and resistors RP and RF. The high-potential end of Vs connects to the non-inverting input of the op-amp at node a. The low-potential end of Vs connects to ground (node e). L, Rf, and the inverting input of the op-amp connect at node b. The other end of RF connects to the output of the op-amp at node c. L and RP connect at node d. The other end of RP connects to ground (node e). Voltage v(t) is measured between node c and ground. Branch current iL(t) flows through L from node d to node b.     Derive a differential equation in terms of . Use KCL at the inverting input of the op-amp.     Use KVL over the inductor and its parasitic resistance.     Plug into , and reduce until the differential equation is in standard form.     Use to derive an equation for .      "
