  "body": "  Derive equations for and given the circuit shown in . The switch has been open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds. The circuit parameters are , , , , and .   Circuit diagram corresponding to .   Circuit with 4 nodes consisting of a voltage source Vs, SPST switch, two resistors, an inductor, and a CCCS ki*iX(t). The high-potential end of Vs connects to the SPST switch at node a. The other end of the SPST switch connects to R1 at node b. R1, R2, the CCCS, and L connect at node c. The CCCS flows into node c. The low-potential end of Vs, R2, L, and the CCCS connect at node d. The CCCS flows out of node d. Voltage v(t) drops over L from node c to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node c to node d. Branch current iX(t) flows through R1 from node b to node c. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.            Analyze the circuit in the final steady-state condition. The inductor can be replaced by a short.     Circuit with 3 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of a voltage source Vs, two resistors, a short, and a CCCS ki*iX(infinity). The high-potential end of Vs connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, the CCCS, and short connect at node b. The CCCS flows into node b. The low-potential end of Vs, R2, the short, and the CCCS connect at node c. The CCCS flows out of node c. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the short from node b to node c. Branch current iX(infinity) flows through R1 from node a to node b.     Resistor is shorted out and can be removed from the circuit.     Circuit with 3 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of a voltage source Vs, one resistor, a short, and a CCCS ki*iX(infinity). The high-potential end of Vs connects to R1 at node a. R1, the CCCS, and short connect at node b. The CCCS flows into node b. The low-potential end of Vs, the short, and the CCCS connect at node c. The CCCS flows out of node c. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the short from node b to node c. Branch current iX(infinity) flows through R1 from node a to node b.     Use KCL at the node connecting the three branches of the circuit.     Use KVL around the outer loop of the circuit.     Use the two equations to solve for .     Analyze the circuit at to calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor. There are two methods that can be used to calculate this value. The first method is to derive the Thévenin equivalent circuit as seen by the inductor.     Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of voltage source Vs, resistors R1 and R2, and CCCS ki*iX(t). The high-potential end of Vs connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, and the CCCS connect at node b. The CCCS flows into node b. The low-potential end of Vs, R2, and the CCCS connect at node c. The CCCS flows out of node c. Branch current iX(t) flows through R1 from node a to node b. The Thevenin equivalent circuit is defined between nodes b and c.     Calculate the open-circuit voltage. Define branch current as the current through resistor .     Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of voltage source Vs, resistors R1 and R2, and CCCS ki*iX(t). The high-potential end of Vs connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, and the CCCS connect at node b. The CCCS flows into node b. The low-potential end of Vs, R2, and the CCCS connect at node c. The CCCS flows out of node c. Branch current iX(t) flows through R1 from node a to node b. Branch current i2(t) flows through R2 from node b to node c. The Thevenin equivalent circuit is defined between nodes b and c.     Use KVL around the left-hand mesh of the circuit to obtain one equation in terms of and .     Use KCL at the node connecting the three branches of the circuit to derive a second equation in terms of and .     The two equations are expressed below with numerical values. The units of V, mA, and kΩ are used, but are not explicitly shown.     The two equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of , the middle column contains coefficients of , and the right column contains constants.     The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.     The open-circuit voltage is equal to .     Short the two terminals of the circuit to calculate the short-circuit current.     Circuit with 3 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of voltage source Vs, resistors R1 and R2, a short, and CCCS ki*iX(t). The high-potential end of Vs connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, the CCCS, and the short connect at node b. The CCCS flows into node b. The low-potential end of Vs, R2, the CCCS, and the short connect at node c. The CCCS flows out of node c. Branch current iX(t) flows through R1 from node a to node b. Branch current Isc is defined through the short from node b to node c.     Resistor is shorted out and can be removed from the circuit.     Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of voltage source Vs, resistor R1, a short, and CCCS ki*iX(t). The high-potential end of Vs connects to R1 at node a. R1, the CCCS, and the short connect at node b. The CCCS flows into node b. The low-potential end of Vs, the CCCS, and the short connect at node c. The CCCS flows out of node c. Branch current iX(t) flows through R1 from node a to node b. Branch current Isc is defined through the short from node b to node c.     Use KVL around the outer loop of the circuit.     Use KCL at the node connecting the three circuit branches.     Use both equations to solve for .     Use Ohm's law to calculate .     The second method is to derive the differential equation from the circuit.     Circuit with 3 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of voltage source Vs, resistors R1 and R2, inductor L, and CCCS ki*iX(t). The high-potential end of Vs connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, the CCCS, and L connect at node b. The CCCS flows into node b. The low-potential end of Vs, R2, the CCCS, and L connect at node c. The CCCS flows out of node c. Branch current iX(t) flows through R1 from node a to node b. Voltage v(t) drops over L from node b to node c. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node b to node c.     Use KVL around the outer loop of the circuit.     Use KCL at the node that connects the four circuit branches. Note that the voltage dropped over resistor is the same as the voltage dropped over the inductor.     Plug into and reduce until the differential equation is in standard form.     This yields the same result as the Thévenin equivalent circuit method, as expected. If we had not already derived the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor, we could use to solve for .  Use to derive an equation for .     Use to derive an equation for .      "
