  "title": "",
  "body": "  Derive equations for and given the circuit shown in . The switch has been open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds. The inductor has a parasitic resistance of 200 Ω, which is not shown on the circuit diagram.   Circuit diagram corresponding to .   Circuit with 4 nodes consisting of an 18A source, three resistors, one inductor, and an SPST switch. The 18A source, 400ohm resistor, and SPST switch connect at node a. The 18A source flows into node a. The SPST switch and 500ohm resistor connect at node b. The 500ohm and 600ohm resistors connect to the 9mH inductor at node c. The 18A source, 400ohm resistor, 600ohm resistor, and 9mH inductor connect at node d. Voltage v(t) drops over the 600ohm resistor from node c to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through the 600ohm resistor from node c to node d. Branch current iL(t) flows through the inductor from node c to node d. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.            In the initial steady-state, the 600 Ω resistor is not connected to any source and has current and voltage values of zero. However, the current through the resistor may not be continuous with time. The initial current through the inductor, , which is also equal to zero, will be continuous when the switch closes. Analyze the circuit at to calculate the value of . The inductor can be treated as a 0 A source. (The parasitic resistance is shown on the diagram below.)     Circuit with 4 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of an 18A source, 0A source, and four resistors. The 18A source, 400ohm and 500ohm resistors connect at node a. The 18A source flows into node a. The 500ohm, 600ohm, and 200ohm resistors connect at node b. The 200ohm resistor connects to the 0A source at node c. The 0A source flows out of node c. The 18A source, 400ohm and 600ohm resistors, and 0A source connect at node d. The 18A source flows out of node d. The 0A source flows into node d. Voltage v(0+) drops over the 600ohm resistor from node b to node d. Branch current i(0+) flows through the 600ohm resistor from node b to node d.     The branch containing the 0 A source does not contribute to the circuit and can be removed.     Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of an 18A source and three resistors. The 18A source, 400ohm and 500ohm resistors connect at node a. The 18A source flows into node a. The 500ohm and 600ohm resistors connect at node b. The 18A source, 400ohm and 600ohm resistors connect at node c. The 18A source flows out of node c. Voltage v(0+) drops over the 600ohm resistor from node b to node c. Branch current i(0+) flows through the 600ohm resistor from node b to node c.     The equivalent resistance of the circuit (as seen by the current source) consists of the 400 Ω resistor in parallel with the series combination of the 500 Ω and 600 Ω resistors. Calculate this equivalent resistance.     Use the current divider rule to calculate .     Analyze the circuit in the final steady-state condition. The inductor can be replaced by its parasitic resistance.     Circuit with 3 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of an 18A source and four resistors. The 18A source, 400ohm and 500ohm resistors connect at node a. The 18A source flows into node a. The 500ohm, 600ohm, and 200ohm resistors connect at node b. The 18A source, 400ohm, 600ohm, and 200ohm resistors connect at node c. The 18A source flows out of node c. Voltage v(infinity) drops over the 600ohm resistor from node b to node c. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the 600ohm resistor from node b to node c.     Use the current divider rule to calculate . Refer to on using the current divider in a three-mesh circuit in if you're uncertain how the equation below was derived.     Analyze the circuit at to calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor. The parasitic resistance is explicitly shown in the diagram below.     Circuit with 4 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of an 18A source, four resistors, and one inductor. The 18A source, 400ohm and 500ohm resistors connect at node a. The 18A source flows into node a. The 500ohm, 600ohm, and 200ohm resistors connect at node b. The 200ohm resistor connects to the 9mH inductor at node c. The 18A source, 400ohm and 600ohm resistors, and 9mH inductor connect at node d. The 18A source flows out of node d. Voltage v(t) drops over the 600ohm resistor from node b to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through the 600ohm resistor from node b to node d.     Deactivate the current source by replacing it with an open.     Circuit with 4 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of an 18A source, four resistors, and one inductor. The 400ohm and 500ohm resistors connect at node a. The 500ohm, 600ohm, and 200ohm resistors connect at node b. The 200ohm resistor connects to the 9mH inductor at node c. The 400ohm and 600ohm resistors and 9mH inductor connect at node d. Voltage v(t) drops over the 600ohm resistor from node b to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through the 600ohm resistor from node b to node d.     Calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor.     Use to calculate .     Use to derive an equation for .     Use Ohm's law to derive an equation for .      "
