  "title": "Review of Important Mathematical Concepts for Second-Order Circuits",
  "body": " Review of Important Mathematical Concepts for Second-Order Circuits  The circuits described in this chapter represent second-order, linear circuits. Analysis of the circuits will yield a second-order, linear, ordinary differential equation with constant coefficients.    Second-order describes the highest-order derivative in the equation. A second-order equation has a second-derivative term and no higher-order derivatives.  Linear describes the operations that occur on the function. If the operations are linear, then the equation is linear.  Ordinary describes the types of derivatives in the equation. If there are no partial derivatives, then the equation is ordinary.    An example of a second-order, linear, ordinary differential equation with constant coefficients is shown in .   In this textbook, we will derive equations in standard form , which means that the coefficient of the highest-order derivative term ( in ) will be equal to one. When in standard form, second-order equations can be described as shown in , where is known as the damping parameter of the circuit and is known as the resonant frequency of the circuit. These two parameters will be described in greater detail in .   When the function as defined in is equal to zero, the equation is said to be homogeneous , otherwise the equation is non-homogeneous. This chapter will cover both constant input and non-constant input non-homogeneous circuits. However, for non-constant input non-homogeneous circuits, we will find that it is much easier to analyze them using the method introduced in .  In order to obtain the exact solution of a second-order, linear, ordinary differential equation, we will need to know the initial value of the function being described by the equation and the initial value of the first derivative of the function being described by the equation . Both of these values must be calculated at a time of , which is important to keep in mind if a quantity is discontinuous with time. If the equation is non-homogeneous, we will also need to know the value of the particular solution.  "
