  "number": "7.2",
  "title": "Circuit Damping",
  "body": " Circuit Damping   As described in , the standard form of the second-order differential equations derived in this textbook (described by where ) will define two important circuit parameters.  Coefficient is used to derive the damping parameter ( ) of the circuit. This quantity describes how quickly the value of voltage or current responds to changes to source values. (If the source is disconnected or reduced, it describes how quickly the output will decay to its final steady-state value. If the source is connected or increased, it describes how quickly the output will rise to its final steady-state value.) The damping parameter is defined in . The unit of is Np\/s (nepers per second). The neper is a dimensionless quantity similar to radians. It is named after John Napier, the inventor of the logarithm.      Summary of the damping parameter    Symbol:  Unit: Np\/s (nepers per second)     Coefficient is used to derive the resonant frequency ( ) of the circuit. The concept of resonance is outside the scope of this textbook. The resonant frequency describes the frequency at which the circuit would have a minimum impedance. (The term impedance will be defined in . For now, it can be considered analogous to resistance.) The resonant frequency is defined in . The unit of is rad\/s.      Summary of the resonant frequency    Symbol:  Unit: rad\/s (radians per second)     When solving a differential equation, the characteristic equation describes the polynomial described by the coefficients of the differential equation. This characteristic equation will yield two roots, and , defined in and . These roots are not usually expressed with a unit. However, because they are multiplied by time in the exponent of an exponential function, it is important to understand that they are not dimensionless quantities.     All second-order circuits with a positive resistance will have negative roots, indicating a stable response to a change in conditions (all exponential terms will converge rather than diverge over time). (Circuit stability is discussed in .)  Based on the value of the roots, there are three different possible output responses that a homogeneous second-order circuit can have. These are: overdamped, underdamped, and critically damped. Each will be explored below.    Overdamped  An overdamped circuit response occurs when . In an overdamped circuit, the voltage or current takes a long time to respond to changes in the circuit conditions. Both of the roots of the characteristic equation ( and ) are real and unique.  A graph depicting the voltage response of an overdamped circuit is shown in . A similar graph could be made for the current in an overdamped circuit. The solid plot represents the source voltage, which abruptly changes from 5 V to 0 V at a time of zero seconds. (This represents a homogeneous circuit.) The dashed line represents an overdamped output voltage corresponding to that change.   Voltage response of an overdamped second-order circuit to an abrupt removal of source voltage.   Graph with time (arbitrary unit) on the x-axis and voltage (V) on the y-axis. In thick black is a function starting at 5V and changing to 0V at a time of 0 and staying 0. The dashed line shows a value of 5V until 0 and then a gradual decay to 0 over time.       Underdamped  An underdamped circuit response occurs when . In an underdamped circuit, the voltage or current responds so quickly to changes in source conditions that the output oscillates around the final value, eventually attenuating to zero. The roots of the characteristic equation ( and ) are complex conjugates (recall that complex conjugates have the form of , where ).  The oscillation frequency ( ) describes the frequency at which the circuit parameter will oscillate. The oscillation frequency is defined in . The unit of the oscillation frequency is rad\/s (radians per second).      Summary of the oscillation frequency    Symbol:  Unit: rad\/s (radians per second)     A graph depicting the voltage response of an underdamped circuit is shown in . A similar graph could be made for the current in an underdamped circuit. The solid plot represents the source voltage, which abruptly changes from 5 V to 0 V at a time of zero seconds. (This represents a homogeneous circuit.) The dashed line represents an underdamped output voltage corresponding to that change.   Voltage response of an underdamped second-order circuit to an abrupt removal of source voltage.   Graph with time (arbitrary unit) on the x-axis and voltage (V) on the y-axis. In thick black is a function starting at 5V and changing to 0V at a time of 0 and staying 0. The dashed line shows a value of 5V until 0 and then a sinusoidal oscillation decaying to 0 over time.       Critically Damped  A critically damped circuit response occurs when . The critically damped circuit has the quickest approach to the steady-state value without oscillating. (In other words, it is the boundary condition between an overdamped and an underdamped circuit.) Both of the roots of the characteristic equation ( and ) are real and equal.  A graph depicting the voltage response of a critically damped circuit is shown in . A similar graph could be made for the current in a critically damped circuit. The solid plot represents the source voltage, which abruptly changes from 5 V to 0 V at a time of zero seconds. (This represents a homogeneous circuit.) The dashed line represents a critically damped output voltage corresponding to that change.   Voltage response of a critically-damped second-order circuit to an abrupt removal of source voltage.   Graph with time (arbitrary unit) on the x-axis and voltage (V) on the y-axis. In thick black is a function starting at 5V and changing to 0V at a time of 0 and staying 0. The dashed line shows a value of 5V until 0 and then a decay to 0 over time.      "
