  "body": " Homogeneous general second-order circuit in symbolic form   Derive a second order differential equation in terms of (in standard form) for the circuit shown below. The switch is closed for a very long time before opening at a time of zero seconds.     Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of a voltage source Vs, resistor Rs, SPST switch, capacitor C, resistor R1, resistor R2, and inductor L. The high-potential end of Vs and Rs connect at node a. Rs and the SPST switch connect at node b. The SPST switch, C, R1, and L connect at node c. L and R2 connect at node d. The low-potential end of Vs, C, R1, and R2 connect at node e. Voltage v(t) drops over R2 from node d to node e. The SPST switch is depicted as opening.     First, analyze the initial conditions of the circuit. The inductor can be replaced by a short and the capacitor can be replaced by an open.     Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of voltage source Vs and resistors Rs, R1, and R2. The high-potential end of Vs and Rs connect at node a. Rs, R1, and R2 connect at node b. The low-potential end of Vs, R1, and R2 connect at node c. Voltage v(0) drops over R2 from node b to node c.     Use a voltage divider to calculate the initial voltage. This value will be continuous with time. This is because resistor is in series with the inductor, and the current through the inductor is continuous with time. Therefore, , which is equal to , will be continuous with time. There will be no need to analyze the circuit at to check for discontinuities.     Analyze the circuit at to derive the second-order equation. Two terms ( and ) are introduced to assist in the analysis.     Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of capacitor C, inductor L, and resistors R1 and R2. C, R1, and L connect at node a. L and R2 connect at node b. C, R1, and R2 connect at node c. Voltage vx(t) drops over C from node a to node c. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node a to node b. Voltage v(t) drops over R2 from node b to node c.     Perform KVL around the loop consisting of the capacitor, inductor, and .     The inductor is in series with resistor . This means that the current through the inductor can be calculated using Ohm's law with . This current is equal to . Plug this quantity into to eliminate the term from the equation.     Perform KCL at the node connecting the capacitor, , and the inductor.     Plug into and reduce to standard form.     Derive equations for alpha and omega.     Derive an equation for the initial derivative of the voltage drop. (Note: this comes from .)     This circuit can now be solved given any numerical values of , , , , and .   "
