  "title": "Constant-Input Non-Homogeneous Second-Order Circuits",
  "body": " Constant-Input Non-Homogeneous Second-Order Circuits   A non-homogeneous second-order circuit means that in the second-order differential equation (described by ) will not be equal to zero.  To solve a non-homogeneous second-order equation, first find the general solution, which is the process used when solving the characteristic equation to calculate the solution to a homogeneous second-order equation. Then, find the particular solution ( ). These two solutions are summed together to derive the overall circuit response. Deriving the particular solution is the only new aspect to solving a constant-input non-homogeneous second-order circuit.  The solutions presented below are universally true for all homogeneous and constant-input non-homogeneous second-order circuits. Homogeneous circuits are simply a special case where or .  The value of the particular solution for constant-input non-homogeneous circuits are given in and .       The Overdamped Solution  The solution to a constant-input non-homogeneous overdamped circuit is described in , with the equation coefficients and defined in and , respectively. Note that this equation is defined with voltage terms. If calculating current, all terms can be replaced by terms.     Coefficients and will have a unit corresponding to the quantity being described by the equation. (Volts for voltage, amps or milliamps for current.) These two coefficients are derived by solving at and by solving the first derivative of at . This derivation is shown in .   Start by solving when .     Calculate the first derivative of and solve when .     Use the equations for and to solve for and .        The Underdamped Solution  The solution to a constant-input non-homogeneous underdamped circuit is described in , with the equation coefficients and defined in and , respectively. Note that this equation is defined with voltage terms. If calculating current, all terms can be replaced by terms.     Coefficients and will have a unit corresponding to the quantity being described by the equation. (Volts for voltage, amps or milliamps for current.) These two coefficients are derived by solving at and by solving the first derivative of at . This derivation is shown in .   Start by solving when . This conveniently derives coefficient .     Calculate the first derivative of and solve when .     Use the equations for and to solve for .        The Critically Damped Solution  The solution to a constant-input non-homogeneous critically damped circuit is described in , with the equation coefficients and defined in and , respectively. Note that this equation is defined with voltage terms. If calculating current, all terms can be replaced by terms.     Coefficient has a unit corresponding to the quantity being described by the equation. (Volts for voltage, amps or milliamps for current.) Coefficient has a unit corresponding to the first derivative of the quantity being described by the equation (volts per second for voltage, or amps per second or milliamps per second for current). These two coefficients are derived by solving at and by solving the first derivative of at . This derivation is shown in .   Start by solving when . This conveniently derives coefficient .     Calculate the first derivative of and solve when .     Use the equations for and to solve for .        Examples   Constant-input non-homogeneous general second-order circuit   Calculate for the circuit below. The switch is open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds. The circuit parameters are , , , , , and .     Circuit with six nodes consisting of voltage source Vs, resistors (Rs, R1, and R2), inductor L, capacitor C, and SPST switch. The high-potential end of Vs connects to Rs at node a. Rs connects to the SPST switch at node b. The SPST switch and L connect at node c. L, R1, and R2 connect at node d. R2 and C connect at node e. The low-potential end of Vs, R1, and C connect at node f. branch current i(t) flows through L from node c to node d. Voltage vX(t) drops over R1 from node d to node f. Voltage v(t) drops over C from node e to node f. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.     The initial parameter values are all equal to zero, as no source is connected to the circuit in the initial conditions. However, it's possible that may not be continuous with time. Analyze the circuit at . The inductor can be replaced by a 0 A current source and the capacitor can be replaced by a 0 V voltage source.     Circuit with two meshes and five nodes consisting of voltage source Vs, resistors (Rs, R1, and R2), a 0V source, and a 0A source. The high-potential end of Vs connects to Rs at node a. Rs and the 0A source connect at node b. The 0A source flows out of node b. The 0A source, R1, and R2 connect at node c. The 0A source flows into node c. R2 and the high-potential end of the 0V source connect at node d. The low-potential end of both voltage sources and R1 connect at node e. vX(0+) drops over R1 from node c to node e.     The 0 A source acts as an open, and the 0 V source acts as a short. Re-draw the circuit.     Circuit with two resistors R1 and R2 connected in series. vX(0+) drops over resistor R1.     The value of is equal to zero.  Analyze the circuit in the final steady-state condition to calculate the particular solution, . The inductor can be replaced by a short and the capacitor can be replaced by an open.     Circuit with one mesh and four nodes consisting of a voltage source Vs and three resistors (Rs, R1, and R2). The high-potential end of Vs and Rs connect at node a. Rs, R1, and R2 connect at node b. R2 floats at node c. The low-potential end of Vs and R1 connect at node d. v(infinity) drops from node c to node d.     Use the voltage divider rule to calculate .     Analyze the circuit at to derive a differential equation.     Circuit with two meshes and five nodes consisting of voltage source Vs, resistors (Rs, R1, and R2), inductor L, and capacitor C. The high-potential end of Vs connects to Rs at node a. Rs and L connect at node b. L, R1, and R2 connect at node c. R2 and C connect at node d. Vs, R1, and C connect at node e. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node b to node c. vX(t) drops over R1 from node c to node e. Voltage v(t) drops over C from node d to node e.     Perform KCL at the node connecting , , and .     Perform KVL around the left-hand loop of the circuit.     Plug into to remove terms.     Perform KVL around the right-hand loop of the circuit. The current through and the capacitor is defined by .     Plug into to remove terms. Then, reduce to standard form.     Plug component values into the differential equation, using base units.     Use to calculate the damping parameter and use to calculate the resonant frequency.     This circuit is underdamped.  Use to calculate . Because both and are zero, will also be zero.  Use to calculate the oscillation frequency of the circuit.     Use and to calculate the coefficients of the equation.     Plug all of these quantities into to obtain the equation for .     The voltage is plotted below as a function of time.     Graph of the equation defined above.        "
