  "body": " Sinusoidal input non-homogeneous second-order circuit (numeric)   Calculate for the circuit below. The switch is open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds. The circuit parameters are , , , , and .     Circuit with five nodes consisting of voltage source vS(t), resistors (Rs and R), SPST switch, capacitor C, and inductor L. The high-potential end of vS(t) connects to Rs at node a. Rs and the SPST switch connect at node b. The SPST switch, R, and C connect at node c. C and L connect at node d. The low-potential end of vS(t), R, and L connect at node e. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node d to node e. Voltage v(t) drops over R from node c to node e. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.     The initial current will be zero, due to the lack of connection to the source. This current will be continuous with time because it's in series with the inductor.  The voltage may not be continuous with time. At , the voltage over the capacitor will be zero, the current through the inductor will be zero, and will be zero (this is because ). This means that , as there are no sources connected to at that time.  Analyze the circuit at to derive the differential equation.     Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source vS(t), resistors (Rs and R), capacitor C, and inductor L. The high-potential end of vS(t) connects to Rs at node a. Rs, R, and C connect at node b. C and L connect at node c. The low-potential end of vS(t), R, and L connect at node d. Voltage v(t) drops over R from node b to node d. Current i(t) flows through L from node c to node d.     Perform KCL at the node connecting , , and and simplify.     Perform KVL around the right-hand loop of the circuit.     Plug into to eliminate all terms.     Take the first derivative of both sides of the equation to eliminate the integral. Then, reduce to standard form.     Analyze the left-hand side of the differential equation. Plug in the known function for and take the derivative as required. The unit is removed from the equation for ease of reading. Base units will be used from this point out.     The differential equation is as shown below.     Plug in component values using base units.     Use to calculate the damping parameter of the circuit and use to calculate the resonant frequency of the circuit.     This circuit is underdamped. Use to calculate the oscillation frequency of the circuit.     It's now time to derive the particular solution. Because the input is a sine wave, the particular solution will take the form given below.     Plug the particular solution into the differential equation. This process will allow us to calculate and .     Differentiate the equation as needed to obtain a function containing entirely sines and cosines without derivatives. Each sine and cosine will be represented as having an argument of to keep the equations from getting too unwieldy.     Collect all terms with cosines from both sides of the equation.     All cosine terms cancel out. Replace all values with to obtain one equation in terms of and .     Collect all terms with sines from both sides of the equation.     All sine terms cancel out. Replace all values with to obtain a second equation in terms of and .     The two equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of , the middle column contains coefficients of , and the right column contains constants.     The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.     This gives us the following two coefficients for the particular solution.     The particular solution is now known.     Because the equation is underdamped, can be used to derive an equation for .     The last step is to calculate the values of coefficients and . This is where the initial conditions and are used. Both of these values are zero. First, use to obtain one equation in terms of and .     Now, use to obtain a second equation in terms of and . (Taking the derivative of the function will not be explicitly shown below.) We can calculate by analyzing and noting that both and the voltage dropped over the capacitor (represented as the integral of in the equation) are equal to zero at . Therefore, .     Using both equations, the two coefficients can be calculated.     Put everything together to express .     The current is plotted below as a function of time. Note that the fast oscillations are caused by the transient response (represented by the general solution), and the slower oscillations are caused by the steady-state response (represented by the particular solution).     Graph of the equation defined above.      "
