  "title": "",
  "body": "  Derive an equation for for the circuit shown in . The switch has been closed for a very long time before opening at a time of zero seconds.   Circuit diagram corresponding to .   Circuit with five nodes consisting of a 10V source, 4kohm resistor, SPST switch, 500mH inductor, 200ohm resistor, and 50uF capacitor. The high-potential end of the 10V source connects to the 4kohm resistor at node a. The 4kohm resistor and SPST switch connect at node b. The SPST switch, 500mH inductor, and 200ohm resistor connect at node c. The 200ohm resistor and 50uF capacitor connect at node d. The low-potential end of the 10V source, 500mH inductor, and 50uF capacitor connect at node e. Branch current iL(t) flows through the inductor from node c to node e. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node d to node e. The SPST switch is depicted as opening.            Calculate the initial conditions of the circuit. The inductor can be replaced by a short, and the capacitor can be replaced by an open.     Circuit with one mesh and four nodes consisting of a 10V source, 4kohm resistor, 200ohm resistor, and a short. The high-potential end of the 10V source connects to the 4kohm resistor at node a. The 4kohm resistor, short, and 200ohm resistor connect at node b. The 200ohm resistor floats at node c. The low-potential end of the 10V source and short connect at node d. Branch current iL(0) flows through the short from node b to node d. Voltage v(0) drops from node c to node d.     Because the initial voltage drop is measured in parallel with the inductor (short), it will be zero. Use Ohm's law to calculate the initial current through the inductor.     Analyze the circuit at .     Circuit with one mesh and three nodes consisting of a 500mH inductor, 200ohm resistor, and 50uF capacitor. The inductor and resistor connect at node a. The resistor and capacitor connect at node b. The inductor and capacitor connect at node c. Branch current iL(t) flows through the inductor from node a to node c. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node b to node c.     This is a homogeneous series RLC circuit.  Use to calculate the initial first derivative of the voltage. Note that the current is negative due to the fact that it opposes the direction of the current through the capacitor, which is defined as flowing from high to low potential.     Use to calculate the damping parameter.     Use to calculate the resonant frequency.     The circuit is critically damped. Use and to calculate the coefficients.     Plug these values into to derive an equation for .      "
