  "title": "",
  "body": "  Derive an equation for for the circuit shown in . The switch is in position a for a very long time before moving to position b at a time of zero seconds.   Circuit diagram corresponding to .   Circuit with seven nodes consisting of a 28V source, 20V source, three resistors (160ohm, 960ohm, and 480ohm), and SPDT switch, 12.5nF capacitor, and 500uH inductor. The high-potential end of the 28V source connects to the 160ohm resistor at node c. The 160ohm resistor connects to position a of the SPDT switch at node a. Position b of the SPDT switch and the 960ohm resistor connect at node b. The input of the SPDT switch, capacitor, and 480ohm resistor connect at node d. The 960ohm resistor, 480ohm resistor, and inductor connect at node e. The inductor and high-potential end of the 20V source connect at node f. The low-potential end of both sources and the capacitor connect at node g. Branch current iL(t) flows through the inductor from node e to node f. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node d to node g. The SPDT switch is depicted as switching from position a to position b.          Draw the circuit in the initial steady-state condition. The capacitor can be replaced by an open and the inductor can be replaced by a short.     Circuit with one mesh and four nodes consisting of a 28V source, 20V source, 160ohm resistor, and 480ohm resistor. The high-potential end of the 28V source connects to the 160ohm resistor at node a. The two resistors connect at node b. The 480ohm resistor connects to the high-potential end of the 20V source at node c. The low-potential end of both sources connect at node d. Branch current iL(0) flows through the 480ohm resistor from node b to node c. Voltage v(0) drops from node b to node d.     Use Ohm's law to calculate the initial current through the inductor.     Use Ohm's law over the 160 Ω resistor to calculate . (Note that you can also calculate this by using Ohm's law over the 480 Ω resistor. Try it out if you want extra practice.)     Analyze the circuit at .     Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of a 20V source, two resistors (960ohm and 480ohm), a 12.5nF capacitor, and 500uH inductor. The capacitor and two resistors connect at node a. The two resistors and inductor connect at node b. The inductor connects to the high-potential end of the 20V source at node c. The capacitor and low-potential end of the 20V source connect at node d. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node a to node d. Branch current iL(t) flows through the inductor from node b to node c.     Simplify the circuit by combining the two parallel resistors.     Circuit with one mesh and four nodes consisting of a 12.5nF capacitor, 320ohm resistor, 500uH inductor, and 20V source. The capacitor and resistor connect at node a. The resistor and inductor connect at node b. The inductor and high-potential end of the 20V source connect at node c. The capacitor and low-potential end of the 20V source connect at node d. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node a to node d. Branch current iL(t) flows through the inductor from node b to node c.     This is a non-homogeneous series RLC circuit. We will need to derive the final steady-state value of the voltage. Draw the circuit in its final steady-state configuration. The capacitor can be replaced by an open and the inductor can be replaced by a short.     Circuit with three nodes consisting of a 320ohm resistor and 20V source. One end of the 320ohm resistor floats at node a. The 320ohm resistor and high-potential end of the 20V source connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 20V source floats at node c. Voltage v(infinity) drops from node a to node c. Branch current iL(infinity) flows through the resistor from node a to node b.     The value of is 20 V.  Draw the circuit in the final steady-state condition. The capacitor can be replaced by an open and the inductor can be replaced by a short.  Use to calculate the damping parameter.     Use to calculate the resonant frequency.     The circuit is underdamped. Use to calculate the oscillation frequency.     Use to calculate the initial first derivative of the voltage drop. Note that the current is negative because the current through the inductor flows in the opposite direction as current through the capacitor (which is defined from high to low potential).     Use and to calculate the coefficients of the underdamped equation.     Plug these values into to derive an equation for .      "
