  "body": "  Derive an equation for for the circuit shown in . The inductor has a parasitic resistance of 10 Ω, which is not explicitly shown in the circuit diagram. The switch is in position a for a very long time before moving to position b at a time of zero seconds.   Circuit diagram corresponding to .   Circuit with five nodes consisting of a 28V source, SPDT switch, 160ohm resistor, 180mH inductor, and 330uF capacitor. The high-potential end of the 28V source connects to the resistor at node c. The 160ohm resistor connects to position a of the SPDT switch at node a. The low-potential end of the 28V source, position b of the SPDT switch, and capacitor connect at node b. The inductor and capacitor connect at node d. The input of the SPDT switch and inductor connect at node f. Branch current iL(t) flows through the inductor from node f to node e. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node d to node b. The SPDT switch is depicted as switching from position a to position b.          Calculate the initial conditions of the circuit. The capacitor can be replaced by an open and the inductor can be replaced by its parasitic resistance.     Circuit with four nodes consisting of a 28V source, 160ohm resistor, and 10ohm resistor. The high-potential end of the 28V source connects to the 160ohm resistor at node a. The two resistors connect at node b. The 10ohm resistor floats at node c. The low-potential end of the 28V source floats at node d. Branch current iL(0) flows through the 10ohm resistor from node b to node c. Voltage v(0) drops from node c to node d.     The initial voltage is equal to 28 V. There is no current through the inductor in this initial steady-state configuration.  Analyze the circuit at .     Circuit with one mesh and two nodes consisting of a 180mH inductor and 330uF capacitor. Both elements connect between nodes a and b. Branch current iL(t) flows through the inductor from node a to node b. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node b to node a.     This is a homogeneous series RLC circuit.  By looking at , we can see that the initial condition for the first derivative of voltage will be equal to zero.  Use to calculate the damping parameter. Be sure to include the parasitic resistance of the inductor in the resistance value in this calculation!     Use to calculate the resonant frequency.     The circuit is underdamped. Use to calculate the oscillation frequency.     Use and to calculate the coefficients.     Plug these values into to derive an equation for .      "
