  "body": "  Derive an equation for given the circuit shown in . The switch is in position a for a very long time before moving to position b at a time of zero seconds. The circuit parameters are , , , and .   Circuit diagram corresponding to .   Circuit with six nodes consisting of a 5V source, 100ohm resistor, SPDT switch, inductor L, capacitor C, and resistors R1 and R2. The high-potential end of the 5V source connects to the 100ohm resistor at node c. The 100ohm resistor connects to position a of the SPDT switch at node a. The low-potential end of the 5V source, position b of the SPDT switch, R1, and C connect at node b. The input of the SPDT switch and L connect at node d. L, R1, and R2 connect at node e. R2 and C connect at node f. Branch current iL(t) flows through L from node e to node d. Voltage vX(t) drops over R1 from node e to node b. Voltage v(t) drops over C from node f to node b. The SPDT switch is depicted as switching from position a to position b.            Analyze the circuit in the initial steady-state condition. The capacitor can be replaced by an open and the inductor can be replaced by a short.     Circuit with one mesh and four nodes consisting of a 5V source, 100ohm resistor, and resistors R1 and R2. The high-potential end of the 5V source connects to the 100ohm resistor at node a. The 100ohm resistor, R1, and R2 connect at node b. R2 floats at node c. The low-potential end of the 5V source and R1 connect at node d. Branch current iL(0) flows through the 100ohm resistor from node b to node a. Voltage vX(0) drops over R1 from node b to node d. Voltage v(0) drops from node c to node d.     Use Ohm's law to calculate . Note the negative sign, which is due to the current direction opposing the source voltage.     Use the voltage divider rule to calculate . This value is also equal to .     Analyze the circuit at .     Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of inductor L, capacitor C, and resistors R1 and R2. L, R1, and R2 connect at node a. R2 and C connect at node b. L, R1, and C connect at node c. Branch current iL(t) flows through L from node a to node c. Voltage vX(t) drops over R1 from node a to node c. Voltage v(t) drops over C from node b to node c.     Solve for the second order differential equation using symbolic form.  Perform KCL at the node connecting , , and .     Use to relate the voltage and current through the inductor.     Plug into to remove terms from .     Take the first derivative of this equation to remove all integrals.     Perform KVL around the right loop. Note that the current through and is defined by because both of those circuit elements are connected in series.     Plug into , and then reduce to standard form.     Plug in component values.     Calculate the damping parameter and resonant frequency.     The circuit is underdamped. Use to calculate the oscillation frequency.     can be used to calculate the initial first derivative of the voltage.     Before plugging in component values, we must first verify that is continuous at . This is because the voltage is not in parallel with a capacitor, so we cannot take for granted that the value will be 1.875 V at . Analyze the circuit at with the capacitor replaced by a 1.875 V voltage source and the inductor replaced by a 31.25 mA current source.     Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of two resistors (R1 and R2), a 31.25mA current source, and 1.875V voltage source. The current source, R1, and R2 connect at node a. the current source flows into node a. R2 and the high-potential end of the voltage source connect at node b. The current source, R1, and the low-potential end of the voltage source connect at node c. The current source flows out of node c. Voltage vX(0+) drops over R1 from node a to node c.     Use KCL at the node connecting the current source, , and to calculate .     Now we can calculate the initial condition of the first derivative of the voltage.     Use and to calculate the coefficients of the underdamped equation.     Plug into to derive the equation for .      "
