  "body": "  Derive an equation for given the circuit shown in . The switch is in position a for a very long time before moving to position b at a time of zero seconds. The circuit parameters are , , , and .   Circuit diagram corresponding to .   Circuit with six nodes consisting of a 4V source, 50ohm resistor, resistors Rd and R, inductor L, capacitor C, and an SPDT switch. The high-potential end of the 4V source connects to the 50ohm resistor at node c. The 50ohm resistor connects to position a of the SPDT switch at node a. Rd connects to position b of the SPDT switch at node b. The input of the SPDT switch, C, and L connect at node d. L and R connect at node e. The low-potential end of the 4V source, Rd, C, and R connect at node f. Branch current i(t) flows through R from node e to node f. Voltage vC(t) drops over C from node d to node f. The SPDT switch is depicted as switching from position a to position b.            Analyze the circuit in its initial steady-state condition. The capacitor can be replaced by an open and the inductor can be replaced by a short.     Circuit with one mesh and three nodes consisting of a 4V source, 50ohm resistor, and resistor R. The high-potential end of the 4V source connects to the 50ohm resistor at node a. The 50ohm resistor and R connect at node b. R and the low-potential end of the 4V source connect at node c. Branch current i(0) flows through R from node b to node c. Voltage vC(0) drops over R from node b to node c.     Use Ohm's law to calculate .     Use the voltage divider rule to calculate .     Analyze the circuit at .     Circuit with two meshes and three nodes consisting of resistors Rd and R, inductor L, and capacitor C. Rd, C, and L connect at node a. L and R connect at node b. Rd, C, and R connect at node c. Branch current i(t) flows through R from node b to node c. Voltage vC(t) drops over C from node a to node c.     Use KCL at the node connecting , , and . Use Ohm's law to derive the current through resistor , and to derive the current through the capacitor.     Use KVL around the right-hand loop of the circuit. Use to derive the voltage dropped over the inductor.     Plug into to obtain a differential equation in terms of . Then, reduce to standard form.     Plug in parameter values using base units.     Calculate the values of the damping parameter and resonant frequency.     The circuit is underdamped. Use to calculate the oscillation frequency.     Use to calculate the initial condition of the first derivative of the current.     Use and to calculate the coefficients.     Plug the values into to obtain an equation for .      "
