  "body": "  Derive an equation for given the circuit shown in . The switch is in position b for a very long time before moving to position a at a time of zero seconds. The component values are , , , , , and .   Circuit diagram corresponding to .   Circuit with five nodes consisting of current source Is, resistors (Rs, R1, and R2), SPDT switch, and capacitors C1 and C2. Is, Rs, and position a of the SPDT switch connect at node a. Is flows into node a. Is, Rs, position b of the SPDT switch, C1, and C2 connect at node b. Is flows out of node b. The input of the SPDT switch and R1 connect at node c. R1, C1, and R2 connect at node d. R2 and C2 connect at node e. Voltage vX(t) drops over C1 from node d to node b. Voltage v(t) drops over C2 from node e to node b. The SPDT switch is depicted as switching from position b to position a.            The initial voltage drops will both be zero as the circuit is not connected to a source in its initial steady-state configuration. Both of these values are continuous with time, as they are voltages measured over capacitors.  To calculate the particular solution, analyze the circuit in its final steady-state condition. Each capacitor can be replaced by an open.     Circuit with one mesh and four nodes consisting of current source Is and resistors Rs, R1, and R2. Is, Rs, and R1 connect at node a. Is flows into node a. R1 and R2 connect at node b. R2 floats at node c. Is and Rs connect at node d. Is flows out of node d. Voltage vX(infinity) drops from node b to node d. Voltage v(infinity) drops from node c to node d.     Neither nor contribute to the circuit in this configuration. Both and are equal to each other. Use Ohm's law to calculate this voltage.     Analyze the circuit at to derive the differential equation.     Circuit with three meshes and four nodes consisting of current source Is, resistors (Rs, R1, and R2) and capacitors C1 and C2. Is, Rs, and R1 connect at node a. Is flows into node a. R1, C1, and R2 connect at node b. R2 and C2 connect at node c. Is, Rs, C1, and C2 connect at node d. Is flows out of node d. Voltage vX(t) drops over C1 from node b to node d. Voltage v(t) drops over C2 from node c to node d.     Transform the current source to a voltage source.     Circuit with two meshes and five nodes consisting of voltage source Is*Rs, resistors Rs, R1, and R2, and capacitors C1 and C2. The high-potential end of the voltage source connects to Rs at node a. Rs and R1 connect at node b. R1, C1, and R2 connect at node c. R2 and C2 connect at node d. The low-potential end of the voltage source, C1, and C2 connect at node e. Voltage vX(t) drops over C1 from node c to node e. Voltage v(t) drops over C2 from node d to node e.     Combine series resistors.     Same circuit as above with Rs and R1 depicted as a single resistor of value Rs+R1.     Perform KCL at the node connecting , , and .     Perform KVL around the right-hand loop of the circuit.     Plus into and reduce to standard form.     Plug in component values, using base units.     Use to calculate the damping parameter and use to calculate the resonant frequency.     can be used to calculate . Because both and are zero, will also be zero.  The circuit is overdamped. Use and to calculate the roots of the characteristic equation.     Use and to calculate the coefficients of the equation.     Plug these values into to derive the equation for .      "
