  "title": "",
  "body": "  Derive an equation for given the circuit shown in . The switch is in position b for a very long time before moving to position a at a time of zero seconds. The component values are , , , , , , and .   Circuit diagram corresponding to .   Circuit with seven nodes consisting of two voltage sources (Vs and Vm*exp(-a*t)), two resistors (R and Rs), and SPDT switch, inductor L, and capacitor C. The high-potential end of Vm*exp(-a*t) connects to R at node c. R connects to position a of the SPDT switch at node a. The high-potential end of Vs connects to Rs at node d. Rs connects to position b of the SPDT switch at node b. The input of the SPDT switch connects to L at node e. L and C connect at node f. The low-potential end of both voltage sources and C connect at node g. Voltage v(t) drops over C from node f to node g. The SPDT switch is depicted as switching from position b to position a.            Analyze the circuit in its initial steady-state configuration to calculate the initial conditions. The inductor can be replaced by a short and the capacitor can be replaced by an open.     Circuit with three nodes consisting of voltage source Vs and resistor Rs. The high-potential end of Vs connects to Rs at node a. Rs floats at node b. The low-potential end of Vs floats at node c. Voltage v(0) drops from node b to node c.     The initial voltage is equal to 8 V. Note that the initial value of the current through the inductor will be 0 A, due to the fact that the circuit is open. Both of these values are continuous with time.  Analyze the circuit at to derive the differential equation.     Circuit with one mesh and four nodes consisting of voltage source Vm*exp(-a*t), resistor R, inductor L, and capacitor C. The high-potential end of the voltage source connects to R at node a. R and L connect at node b. L and C connect at node c. The low-potential end of the voltage source connects to c at node d. Voltage v(t) drops over C from node c to node d.     This is a series RLC circuit, so we should expect the characteristic equation to resemble what we derived in . Knowing that the current through the circuit is defined by , we can simply perform KVL around the entire circuit loop. Then, reduce until the differential equation is in standard form.     Plug in parameter values, using base units.     Use to calculate the damping parameter and use to calculate the resonant frequency.     The circuit is critically damped.  The particular solution has a format of , where . Plug this into the differential equation and solve to calculate .     The equation for has the following form.     Use and to calculate and . The first derivative is defined by , which will be equal to zero.     Plug all of the values into the equation for .      "
