  "number": "7.8.18",
  "title": "",
  "body": "  Derive an equation for given the circuit shown in . The switch is open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds. The circuit parameters are , , , , and .   Circuit diagram corresponding to .   Circuit with five nodes consisting of voltage source vAC(t), resistors Rs and R, SPST switch, inductor L, and capacitor C. The high-potential end of vAC(t) connects to Rs at node a. Rs and the SPST switch connect at node b. The SPST switch, R, and L connect at node c. L and C connect at node d. The low-potential end of vAC(t), R, and C connect at node e. Voltage vX(t) drops over R from node c to node e. Voltage v(t) drops over C from node d to node e. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.            The initial value of will be zero due to the lack of any source in the initial steady-state condition. The initial current through the inductor will also be zero. Both of these values are continuous with time.  The voltage over the resistor may not be continuous with time. At , the value of the source, , will be equal to zero. (This is because .) Therefore, will also be equal to zero.  Analyze the circuit at to derive the differential equation.     Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of voltage source vAC(t), resistors Rs and R, inductor L, and capacitor C. The high-potential end of vAC(t) connects to Rs at node a. Rs, R, and L connect at node b. L and C connect at node c. The low-potential end of vAC(t), R, and C connect at node d. Voltage vX(t) drops over R from node b to node d. Voltage v(t) drops over C from node c to node d.     Perform KCL at the node connecting , , and . Combine like terms.     Perform KVL around the right-hand loop of the circuit. Note that the current through the inductor is equal to .     Plug into and simplify to standard form.     Plug in parameter values, using base units.     Use to calculate the damping parameter, and use to calculate the resonant frequency.     This circuit is underdamped. Use to calculate the oscillation frequency of the circuit.     Use to calculate . Because , , and are all equal to zero, will also be equal to zero.  Refer to to determine the form of the particular solution.     Plug the particular solution into the differential equation and combine like terms.     All of the sine terms can be used to generate one equation relating and .     All of the cosine terms can be used to generate a second equation relating and .     The two equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of , the middle column contains coefficients of , and the right column contains constants.     The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.     The coefficient can be rounded to zero. The particular form of the solution is now known.     The equation for has the following form.     Use and to calculate and . Start with .     The equation for now has the following form.     Take the first derivative of the above expression, and set equal to at a time of to calculate .     The equation for is now known.     A graph of is shown below. The high-frequency oscillations correspond to the AC voltage source (particular solution). The low-frequency oscillations correspond to the transient response (general solution).     Graph of the equation defined above.      "
