  "id": "ch8-impedance",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "ch8-impedance.html",
  "type": "Section",
  "number": "8.2",
  "title": "Impedance",
  "body": " Impedance   Impedance is the phasor equivalent of resistance and is used in AC circuits. It relates to the measure of opposition to current based on a given voltage rise or drop. The unit of impedance is the same as that of resistance, Ω (ohm).   Summary of impedance    Symbol:  Unit: Ω (ohm)     Using time-varying signals, . Using phasors, the relationship between voltage, current, and impedance is defined by complex Ohm's law, defined in .     Because impedance is a complex quantity, it can be represented in cartesian form, shown in . The quantity is the resistance of the circuit element (this is the same resistance that we've been discussing in this textbook since ), and is the reactance of the circuit element.      Summary of reactance    Symbol:  Unit: Ω (ohm)       Impedance in Resistors  In a resistor, voltage and current are related via Ohm's law (introduced in ). The impedance of a resistor is defined in , where is the resistance of the resistor. (There is no difference in the way we express resistance in AC circuits as compared to DC circuits.)     If the voltage drop over a resistor is defined as , then Ohm's law states that the current through the resistor will be equal to . The calculation of the impedance of a resistor is derived in .         Impedance in Capacitors  The impedance of a capacitor is defined in , where is the capacitance of the capacitor.     Because is in the denominator of the impedance equation, the reactance of a capacitor will always be negative .  The impedance of a capacitor is purely imaginary (mathematically) and is frequency dependent. As the frequency of a circuit approaches zero, the impedance of a capacitor approaches infinity. This explains why we were able to analyze capacitors in steady-state DC conditions as open circuits. As the frequency of a circuit approaches infinity, the impedance of a capacitor becomes zero, making it act as a short circuit.  In a capacitor, the relationship between voltage and current is defined by . The calculation of the impedance of a capacitor is derived in .         Impedance in Inductors  The impedance of an inductor is defined in , where is the inductance of the inductor.     Because is in the numerator of the impedance equation, the reactance of an inductor will always be positive .  The impedance of an inductor is purely imaginary (mathematically) and is frequency dependent. As the frequency of a circuit approaches zero, the impedance of an inductor also approaches zero. This explains why we were able to analyze inductors in steady-state DC conditions as short circuits. As the frequency of a circuit approaches infinity, the impedance of an inductor also approaches infinity, making it act as an open circuit.  In an inductor, the relationship between voltage and current is defined by . The calculation of the impedance of an inductor is derived in .         Equivalent Impedance  Impedance takes a lot of confusion out of simplifying circuits. Impedances in series always add together, as shown in .     The reciprocal sum is used to calculate parallel impedances, as shown in .     Deriving equivalent impedance enables us to simplify circuits containing resistors, capacitors, and inductors, which we were unable to do in previous chapters.   Equivalent impedance   Calculate the equivalent impedance of the circuit shown below, and express the simplified circuit with individual components. The frequency of the circuit is 100 Hz.     Circuit with one mesh and five nodes consisting of two resistors (20ohm and 50ohm), two capacitors (70uF and 65uF), and one 65mH inductor. The 20ohm resistor floats at node a. The 20ohm and 70uF connect at node b. The 70uF, 65mH, and 50ohm connect at node c. The 50ohm and 65uF connect at node d. he 65mH and 65uF connect at node e. The equivalent impedance is measured between nodes a and e.     Calculate the impedance of the 70 µF capacitor.     Calculate the impedance of the 65 µF capacitor.     Calculate the impedance of the inductor.     Re-draw the circuit.     Circuit with one mesh and five nodes consisting of five impedances (20ohm, 50ohm, -j22.7ohm, -j24.5ohm, and j40.9ohm). The 20ohm resistor floats at node a. The 20ohm and -j22.7ohm connect at node b. The -j22.7ohm, j40.9ohm, and 50ohm connect at node c. The 50ohm and -j24.5ohm connect at node d. he j40.9ohm and -j24.5ohm connect at node e. The equivalent impedance is measured between nodes a and e.     Combine all series impedances. The 20 Ω resistor is in series with the j22.74 Ω capacitor. The 50 Ω resistor is in series with the j24.49 Ω capacitor.     Circuit with one mesh and three nodes consisting of 3 impedances (20-j22.7ohm, j40.8ohm, and 50-j24.5ohm). The 20-j22.74ohm floats at node a. The 20-j22.7ohm, j40.8ohm, and 50-j24.5ohm connect at node b. The j40.8ohm and 50-j24.5ohm connect at node c. The equivalent impedance is measured between nodes a and c.     Two of the impedances (j40.84 Ω and 50 j24.49 Ω) are connected in parallel and can be reduced to an equivalent impedance of 30.13+j30.98 Ω.     Circuit with three nodes consisting of 2 impedances (20-j22.7ohm, and 30.1+j31ohm). The 20-j22.7ohm floats at node a. The 20-j22.7ohm and 30.1+j31ohm connect at node b. The 30.1+j31ohm floats at node c. The equivalent impedance is measured between nodes a and c.     The last two impedances can be combined in series to an equivalent impedance of 50.13+j8.25 Ω. This impedance consists of a resistor (50.1 Ω) and an inductor. (We know it must be an inductor because the reactance is positive.) The value of that inductor can be calculated using .     The equivalent circuit is depicted below.     Circuit consisting of a 50.1ohm resistor in series with a 13.1mH inductor.        "
