  "title": "Delta-Wye and Wye-Delta Transforms",
  "body": " Delta-Wye and Wye-Delta Transforms  The concept of delta-wye and wye-delta transforms was discussed in . Just as we could do delta-wye and wye-delta transforms for resistive circuits, so we can do them with circuits containing resistors, capacitors, and inductors. These transforms can be used to find the equivalent impedance of circuit elements that are neither in parallel nor in series with each other.  A delta circuit arrangement of impedances is shown in .   A delta arrangement of impedances.   Three impedances (Z1, Z2, and Z3) arranged in series in a triangle. The node between Z1 and Z3 is a. The node between Z1 and Z2 is b. The node between Z2 and Z3 is c.     The impedance between any two nodes in a delta circuit are parallel and series combinations of those impedances, shown in , , and .     A wye circuit arrangement of impedances is shown in .   A wye arrangement of impedances.   Three impedances (Za, Zb, and Zc) arranged in a Y shape. All three impedances connect at a common node (not labeled). The dangling end of Za is labeled \"a,\" the dangling end of Zb is labeled \"b,\" and the dangling end of Zc is labeled \"c.\"     The impedance between any two nodes in a wye circuit are series combinations of those impedances, shown in , , and .     A delta-wye transform modifies a delta arrangement of impedances ( ) and converts it into an equivalent wye arrangement ( ). The equivalent impedance between any two nodes of the wye circuit must be identical to the corresponding equivalent impedance of the delta circuit in order to make this transformation valid. is set equal to ; is set equal to ; and is set equal to . The quantities , , and are solved for. (The algebraic details of these derivations will not be shown in this book.)  The impedances in a wye circuit derived after a delta-wye transform are given in , , and .      Using a delta-wye transformation to calculate equivalent impedance   Calculate the equivalent impedance of the circuit as shown, and express as individual components. The frequency of the circuit is 1 kHz.     Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of three resistors (400ohm, 500ohm, and 1000ohm), one 500nF capacitor, and one 20mH inductor. The 400ohm and 500nF connect at node a. The 400ohm, 20mH, and 500ohm connect at node b. The 500nF, 20mH, and 1000ohm connect at node c. The 500ohm and 1000ohm connect at node d. The equivalent impedance is measured between nodes a and d.     Calculate the impedance of the capacitor.     Calculate the impedance of the inductor.     None of the circuit elements shares a branch, therefore none are in series. None of the circuit elements share two nodes, therefore none are in parallel. A delta-wye transform is required. (Note that a wye-delta transform could be accomplished as well.)  The circuit has been re-drawn. The impedances in the delta configuration are highlighted in red, with each of the three nodes labeled.     Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of five impedances (400ohm, 500ohm, 1000ohm, j126ohm, and -j318ohm). The 400ohm and -j318ohm connect at node a. The 400ohm, j125.66ohm, and 500ohm connect at node b. The -j318ohm, j126ohm, and 1000ohm connect at node c. The 500ohm and 1000ohm connect at node d. The equivalent impedance is measured between nodes a and d. The delta circuit consists of the 500ohm resistor, 1000ohm resistor, and j126ohm impedance.     Use , , and to calculate for , , and .     Re-draw the circuit with the wye impedances.     Circuit with one mesh and five nodes consisting of five impedances (400ohm, -j318ohm, 3.48+j41.6ohm, 6.97+j83.2ohm, and 331-j27.7ohm). The 400ohm and -j318ohm connect at node a. The 400ohm and 3.48+j41.6ohm connect at node b. The -j318ohm and 6.97+j83.2ohm connect at node c. The 3.48+j41.6ohm, 6.97+j83.2ohm, and 331-j27.7ohm connect at node d. The 331-j27.7ohm floats at node e. The equivalent impedance is measured between nodes a and e.     Combine all series impedances.     Circuit with one mesh and three nodes consisting of three impedances (403+j41.6ohm, 6.97-j235ohm, and 331-j27.7ohm). The 403+j41.6ohm and 6.97-j235ohm connect at node a. All three impedances connect at node b. The 331-j27.7ohm floats at node c. The equivalent impedance is measured between nodes a and c.     Calculate the equivalent impedances of the two upper circuit elements that are in parallel with each other.     The overall equivalent impedance is equal to the above impedance added to 331.01 j27.73 Ω.     This impedance is consistent of a resistor in series with a capacitor. (We know it must be a capacitor because the reactance is negative.) Use to calculate the value of the capacitor.        Circuit consisting of a 445ohm resistor in series with a 779nF capacitor.       A wye-delta transform modifies a wye arrangement of impedances ( ) and turns it into an equivalent delta arrangement ( ). Just as with the delta-wye transforms, the circuits must be equivalent to each other. is set equal to ; is set equal to ; and is set equal to . The quantities , , and are solved for. (The algebraic details of these derivations will not be shown in this book.)  The impedances in a delta circuit derived after a wye-delta transform are given in , , and .      Using a wye-delta transformation to calculate equivalent impedance   Calculate the equivalent impedance of the circuit as shown, and express as individual components. The frequency of the circuit is 200 Hz.     Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of three resistors (400ohm, 600ohm, and 800ohm), one 200mH inductor, and one 2uF capacitor. The 400ohm and 600ohm connect at node a. The 400ohm, 200mH, and 2uF connect at node b. The 600ohm, 200mH, and 800ohm connect at node c. The 2uF and 800ohm connect at node d. The equivalent impedance is measured between nodes a and d.     Calculate the impedance of the capacitor.     Calculate the impedance of the inductor.     None of these elements shares a branch, therefore none are in series. None of these elements shares two nodes, therefore none are in parallel. A wye-delta transform is required. (Note that a delta-wye transform could be accomplished as well.)  The circuit has been re-drawn. The impedances in the wye-arrangement are highlighted in red, with each of the three nodes labeled.     Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of five impedances (400ohm, 600ohm, 800ohm, j251ohm, and -j398ohm). The 400ohm and 600ohm connect at node a. The 400ohm, j251ohm, and -j398ohm connect at node b. The 600ohm, j251ohm, and 800ohm connect at node c. The -j398ohm and 800ohm connect at node d. The equivalent impedance is measured between nodes a and d.     Use , , and to solve for , , and .     Re-draw the circuit.     Circuit with three meshes and three nodes consisting of five impedances (600ohm, 800ohm, 147+j251ohm, 250-j147ohm, and -233-j398ohm). The 600ohm, 147+j251ohm, and -233-j398ohm connect at node a. The 600ohm, 147+j251ohm, 250-j147ohm, and 800ohm connect at node b. The -233-j398ohm, 250-j147ohm, and 800ohm connect at node c. The equivalent impedance is measured between nodes a and c.     Calculate the equivalent impedance of the 600 Ω resistor in parallel with the 147.34+j251.33 Ω impedance.     Calculate the equivalent impedance of the 800 Ω resistor in parallel with the 250 j146.56 Ω impedance.     Re-draw the circuit.     Circuit with one mesh and three nodes consisting of three impedances (167+j146ohm, -233-j398ohm, and 202-j83.5ohm). The -233-j398ohm and 167+j146ohm connect at node a. The 167+j146ohm and 202-j83.5ohm connect at node b. The -233-j398ohm and 202-j83.5ohm connect at node c. The equivalent impedance is measured between nodes a and c.     The equivalent impedance is calculated below.     This impedance is equivalent to a resistor in series with a capacitor. (We know it must be a capacitor because the reactance is negative.) Use to calculate the value of the capacitor.        Circuit with a 349ohm resistor in series with a 2.45uF capacitor.       "
